var SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture Overview", "subtitle": "System design of the Python / PySide6 backend and the QML frontend it drives: communication patterns,\n            startup order, threading model, observability, and the reasoning behind those choices \u2014 verified\n            against main.py and the modules in backend/.", "headings": [{"id": "high-level", "text": "High-Level Architecture", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-frontend", "text": "Backend / Frontend Split", "level": 2}, {"id": "manager-pattern", "text": "The Manager Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "all-managers", "text": "All Managers", "level": 3}, {"id": "communication", "text": "Backend-QML Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Context Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 3}, {"id": "properties", "text": "Qt Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "qml-types", "text": "Custom QML Types", "level": 3}, {"id": "startup", "text": "Application Startup Sequence", "level": 2}, {"id": "startup-python", "text": "main.py, step by step", "level": 3}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "thread-obd", "text": "OBD Worker Thread", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-pool", "text": "Thread Pools and Polled Futures", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-sensors", "text": "Sensor Read Loops", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-watchdog", "text": "UI Watchdog", "level": 3}, {"id": "volume-system", "text": "Volume System", "level": 2}, {"id": "video", "text": "Video into QML: Image Provider vs QVideoSink", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-server", "text": "The Phone Server Fork", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-persistence", "text": "Settings Persistence", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboards", "text": "Dashboards: JSON Specs + DashboardManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-3d", "text": "The 3D Vehicle (CarMenu)", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging, Crash Handlers and Diagnostics", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tooling", "text": "Profiling and the Command Server", "level": 2}, {"id": "signal-wiring", "text": "Signal Wiring Graph", "level": 2}, {"id": "cleanup", "text": "Shutdown &amp; Cleanup", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions &mdash; Why It Is Built This Way", "level": 2}], "body": "Architecture - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Architecture Architecture Overview System design of the Python / PySide6 backend and the QML frontend it drives: communication patterns, startup order, threading model, observability, and the reasoning behind those choices \u2014 verified against main.py and the modules in backend/ . High-Level Architecture OCTAVE is a backend/frontend split joined by Qt's signals and slots. The Python / PySide6 backend in backend/ + main.py owns all business logic, hardware I/O, network calls and data processing; the QML frontend in frontend/ owns rendering, animation and interaction. The backend runs on desktop platforms only (Linux, Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi, Windows, macOS). \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 OCTAVE Application \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 Signals / Slots \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Python backend \u2502 \u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba \u2502 QML Frontend \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 backend/ + main.py \u2502 Context Props \u2502 frontend/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 SettingsManager Clock \u2502 \u2502 Main.qml \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DiagnosticsManager \u2502 \u2502 MainMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 VolumeController \u2502 \u2502 MediaRoom \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 MediaManager AudioAnalyzer\u2502 \u2502 MediaPlayer \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 OBDManager (python-obd) \u2502 \u2502 OBDMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 OBDAdapterFinder \u2502 \u2502 dashboards/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 NetworkManager \u2502 \u2502 gauges/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DownloadManager \u2502 \u2502 CarMenu + \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 ESP32VolumeManager \u2502 \u2502 JeepVehicle \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 BerryIMUManager \u2502 \u2502 SensorMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 GestureManager \u2502 \u2502 Settings/* \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 AndroidAutoManager \u2502 \u2502 BottomBar \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 PhoneMirrorManager \u2502 \u2502 ... \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DashboardManager AssetStore \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 UiWatchdog (no QML API) \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 External video \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 embedded DHU window (Windows) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 QVideoSink (Phone Mirror)\u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 PySide6 runtime \u2014 QApplication \u2192 QQmlApplicationEngine \u2192 exec() \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 Backend / Frontend Split Layer Technology Location Responsibility Backend Python 3.10+ / PySide6 6.8+ backend/ Business logic, hardware I/O, API calls, data processing, state. Runs from a checkout; never packaged. Frontend QML (Qt Quick, Qt Quick 3D for the vehicle view) frontend/ UI rendering, animation, interaction, layout \u2014 loaded from disk at runtime Bridge main.py repo root Instantiates managers, registers context properties, the custom type and the image provider, wires cross-manager signals, owns shutdown Managers own hardware access and application state; QML consumes that state through the registered API. A few integrations cross the rendering boundary on purpose: the embedded Android Auto window, the phone mirror's video sink, and the developer-local command server that drives the UI. The contract between the two layers is the set of context-property names, signals, slots and properties. Where QML branches on isAndroid , the branch is never taken here: main.py sets it to False . The Manager Pattern Every backend feature is a manager class inheriting QObject that exposes state through PySide6's Property , Slot and Signal : # backend/example_manager.py (illustrative) from PySide6.QtCore import QObject, Signal, Slot, Property class ExampleManager (QObject): valueChanged = Signal( int ) # QML listens to these def __init__ (self): super ().__init__() self._value = 0 @Slot( int ) # callable from QML def setValue (self, v): self._value = v self.valueChanged.emit(v) @Property( int , notify=valueChanged) # bindable from QML def value (self): return self._value Key principles: Signals notify the UI (and other managers) when backend state changes Slots are methods QML can call; names follow what the QML calls, so camelCase and snake_case both occur ( applyVolume , next_track , scan_library ) Properties expose values with automatic binding updates Managers are self-contained : each owns its state, timers and threads and releases them in cleanup() (or close() for OBD) main.py wires cross-manager signals and passes dependencies: Clock(settings_manager) , connect_settings_manager(settings_manager) on the media, ESP32, BerryIMU, gesture and download managers, the output managers handed to VolumeController , and OBDAdapterFinder.set_obd_manager() All Managers Every row is instantiated in main.py and (except the watchdog) registered on the root context. Class Module ( backend/ ) Context property Purpose SettingsManager settings_manager.py settingsManager Central settings store, JSON persistence; module function get_app_data_dir() DiagnosticsManager diagnostics_manager.py diagnosticsManager Read / copy / export the log files from the head unit (Settings \u2192 Device \u2192 Diagnostics) Clock clock.py clock Formatted time string, format from settings AssetStore asset_store.py assetStore localUrl(relativePath) \u2192 file:// URL under frontend/assets/ (3D models)"}, {"url": "audio-analyzer.html", "title": "Audio Analyzer", "subtitle": "FFT waveform visualization in backend/audio_analyzer.py: PyAV decoding, numpy FFT, the 96-bar layout, live phone PCM, and the data flow from file to QML", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Pipeline Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "module", "text": "Module and Worker", "level": 2}, {"id": "bar-count", "text": "Bar Count and the Single Toggle", "level": 2}, {"id": "decoding", "text": "Audio Decoding", "level": 2}, {"id": "fft-pipeline", "text": "FFT Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "frequency-bands", "text": "Frequency Band Distribution", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-flow", "text": "Data Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "live-audio", "text": "Live Phone Audio", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-integration", "text": "QML Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Considerations", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Dependencies and Graceful Degradation", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Audio Analyzer - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Audio Analyzer Audio Analyzer FFT waveform visualization in backend/audio_analyzer.py: PyAV decoding, numpy FFT, the 96-bar layout, live phone PCM, and the data flow from file to QML Pipeline Overview backend/audio_analyzer.py pre-computes FFT visualization data for a whole audio track (up to 3 hours) on a worker thread, then serves time-indexed levels to QML from the playback position. Local-file playback uses this pre-computed data; mirrored-phone audio uses the separate live PCM path . Both produce 96 bars on a 0-8 integer scale from 100 ms windows. Audio File (.mp3/.flac/.m4a/...) | v +--------------------------------------+ | PyAV: av.open(), first audio stream, | | container.decode(), frame by frame | +--------------------------------------+ | each frame on arrival v +------------------+ | Preprocessing | Average channels to mono (float32) | | Decimate to ~8 kHz (every Nth sample of the whole | | track; phase carried across frames) +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | FFT Analysis | 100 ms chunks, FFT'd every 600 chunks (60 s) | numpy.fft.rfft | Hann window, magnitudes, DC dropped | | 96 log-spaced bands (each >= 1 bin), mean per band | | Only band magnitudes kept +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Normalization | 95th percentile of all non-zero band values = 1.0 | & Quantization | Power curve (^0.6) | | int(norm * 8) -> 0..8 +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | _fft_data | (num_chunks, 96) uint8 numpy array, | | 10 rows per second of audio +------------------+ | v (during playback, 16 ms timer in MediaRoom) +------------------+ | update_position | chunk = int(seconds * 10); zeros past the end | | Emit fftDataChanged only if levels changed +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | QML Visualizer | 96 Rectangles, height = level / 8 | (MediaRoom.qml) | Behavior animations smooth the steps +------------------+ Module and Worker Piece Implementation Class AudioAnalyzer(QObject) , instantiated in main.py and registered as the context property audioAnalyzer Decoder PyAV ( av , FFmpeg bindings) FFT numpy.fft.rfft along the chunk axis Worker concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) , collected by a 100 ms QTimer on the GUI thread Live input phone_mirror_manager.audioPlayed connected to audio_analyzer.feed_live_pcm in main.py The only consumer is the waveform visualizer in frontend/MediaRoom.qml . Bar Count and the Single Toggle The visualizer renders a fixed 96 frequency bars ( _num_bars = 96 , returned by get_num_bars() ) and MediaRoom polls every 16 ms. The only setting is showWaveformVisualizer (default true, see Settings Reference ); there are no quality tiers. The MediaRoom container hard-codes its look as properties: timerInterval: 16 , animDuration: 120 , animEasing: Easing.InOutSine , smoothBars: true and pill-shaped fancyBars: true . Audio Decoding _analyze_audio(file_path) opens the file with av.open() and takes the first stream whose type is 'audio' ; supported inputs depend on the codecs in PyAV's FFmpeg build. The decode is streamed: each frame is converted to an ndarray, mixed to mono and decimated as it arrives, so nothing at the native rate is kept beyond the current frame. Planar frames are (channels, n) ; packed (interleaved) frames are (1, n \u00d7 channels) and are reshaped by the layout's channel count before averaging: container = av. open (file_path) audio_stream = next ((s for s in container.streams if s.type == 'audio' ), None ) for frame in container. decode (audio_stream): data = frame. to_ndarray () channels = len (frame.layout.channels) if data.ndim > 1 and data.shape[ 0 ] > 1 : # planar (channels, n) mono = data. mean (axis= 0 , dtype=np.float32) elif channels > 1 : # packed, interleaved mono = data. reshape (- 1 , channels). mean (axis= 1 , dtype=np.float32) else : mono = data. reshape (- 1 ). astype (np.float32) piece = mono[(-position) % factor::factor] # decimate, phase kept across frames position += len (mono) pending. append (piece) # FFT'd every ANALYSIS_BATCH_CHUNKS chunks The FFT is vectorised per batch: every ANALYSIS_BATCH_CHUNKS = 600 chunks (60 s of audio) the pending decimated samples are concatenated, cut to a whole number of chunks, reshaped to (n, chunk_size) , Hann-windowed and transformed with one np.fft.rfft(..., axis=1) ; band means come from np.add.reduceat , and only the resulting band magnitudes are kept. A remainder shorter than a chunk carries over to the next batch and is dropped at the end. Normalisation and quantisation at the end are array operations. This keeps Python-level loops, which hold the GIL, out of the per-sample work; the worker still shares the GIL with the GUI thread, so a very long analysis can be seen in the perf monitor's GIL jitter. Preprocessing Mix to mono - per frame, as above, in float32 Decimate - keep every Nth sample with N = max(1, sample_rate // 8000) ( sample_rate is the stream's rate, or 44100 if unset), so a 44.1 kHz file becomes 88"}, {"url": "bottom-bar.html", "title": "Bottom Bar", "subtitle": "The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, dual orientation, volume routing through VolumeController, navigation button behaviour, the clock, and the update-available indicator", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "layout", "text": "Layout and Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "orientation", "text": "Dual Orientation", "level": 2}, {"id": "media-controls", "text": "Media Transport Controls", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume", "text": "Volume Slider and Mute", "level": 2}, {"id": "mute-states", "text": "Mute Icon States", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation", "text": "Navigation Buttons", "level": 2}, {"id": "active-highlight", "text": "Active Page Highlight", "level": 3}, {"id": "update-notification", "text": "Update Notification", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock Section", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle", "text": "Shuffle State", "level": 2}, {"id": "external-volume", "text": "External Volume Sources", "level": 2}, {"id": "managers", "text": "Manager Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "visibility-popup", "text": "Toggle View Popup", "level": 2}, {"id": "sizing", "text": "Sizing Tokens", "level": 2}, {"id": "animation-pattern", "text": "Button Animation Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "svg-pattern", "text": "SVG Icon Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Bottom Bar - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Bottom Bar Bottom Bar The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, dual orientation, volume routing through VolumeController, navigation button behaviour, the clock, and the update-available indicator Overview frontend/BottomBar.qml is the persistent control surface that is visible on every page. It is a Rectangle with a themed gradient background whose contents are provided by a Loader that swaps between two complete layout components: a horizontal bar docked at the bottom of the window and a vertical bar docked at the left. Both layouts carry the same three logical sections \u2014 media transport controls, navigation buttons, and the clock. It is instantiated once in frontend/Main.qml with z: 0 ; the page StackView sits above it ( z: 1 ) and anchors to bottomBar.top (horizontal) or bottomBar.right (vertical). A 12 px gradient Rectangle ( bottomBarShadow / sideBarShadow ) in Main.qml draws a soft shadow along the bar's inner edge. Horizontal layout (bottomBarOrientation = \"bottom\"): +-----------------------------+-----------------------------------+--------------+ | SECTION 1 (40 % width) | SECTION 2 (fills remaining) | SECTION 3 | | Media controls | Navigation | (20 % width) | | [<<] [>||] [>>] [Shuf] [Mute] 50% [Home] [OBD] [Media] [Sensor] [Gear] [AA] [PM] | 12:34 | +-----------------------------+-----------------------------------+--------------+ Vertical layout (bottomBarOrientation = \"side\", 10 % of window width): +----------+ | [<<][>||][>>] SECTION 1 (top 40 % height) | [Shuf] | | [Mute] | | 50% | |----------| | [Home] | SECTION 2 (fills remaining) | [OBD] | | [Media] | | [Sensor] | | [Gear] | | [AA] | | [PM] | |----------| | 12:34 | SECTION 3 (bottom 10 % height) +----------+ Layout and Structure The root is a Rectangle whose gradient direction follows the orientation. Note the unusual stop positions \u2014 the \"start\" colour is placed at 1.5 , beyond the end of the gradient, so the visible bar is dominated by bottomBarGradientEnd (the theme's baseAlt ) fading toward base : Rectangle { id : bottomBar gradient : Gradient { orientation : isVertical ? Gradient.Horizontal : Gradient.Vertical GradientStop { position : 1.5 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientStart } GradientStop { position : 0.0 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientEnd } } required property StackView stackView required property Window mainWindow Loader { anchors.fill : parent sourceComponent : isVertical ? verticalLayoutComponent : horizontalLayoutComponent } } Like every other QML file in the project, the bar defines local dp() / dpMin() wrappers ( Math.round(size * (App.Spacing.effectiveScale || 1.0)) ) instead of calling App.Spacing.dp() . The comment at the top of the file explains why: calling functions on a QML singleton is unreliable on Android, so every file re-implements the two-line helper locally. Under main.py the wrapper and App.Spacing.dp() give the same result. Section 1: Media Controls In the horizontal layout this Item takes parent.width * 0.4 when settingsManager.showBottomBarMediaControls is true. When the user hides the media controls the item stays in the layout but its width is bound to clockSection.width , so the navigation row still sits at the true horizontal centre. The vertical layout uses the same trick with heights (40 % shown, 10 % hidden). Section 2: Navigation Uses Layout.fillWidth: true (or fillHeight when vertical) and centres a RowLayout / ColumnLayout of nav buttons. The horizontal row has z: 1 so that, with many buttons enabled, its outer buttons (Phone Mirror in particular) still receive clicks when the row is wider than its section. Its spacing is computed by fitSpacing : readonly property real preferredSpacing: App.Spacing.bottomBarBetweenButtonMargin * 6 readonly property real fitSpacing: { var n = 0 , w = 0 for ( var i = 0 ; i < children.length; ++i) { var c = children[i] if (c.visible && c.implicitWidth > 0 ) { n++; w += c.implicitWidth } } if (n < 2 ) return preferredSpacing var avail = parent.width - w return Math. max ( dp ( 4 ), Math. min (preferredSpacing, avail / (n - 1 ))) } The gap between buttons shrinks (down to dp(4) ) instead of letting the row overflow under the clock or the media controls. Section 3: Clock Horizontal: parent.width * 0.2 when settingsManager.showClock is true, otherwise 0 so the navigation row re-centres. Vertical: a fixed 10 % of the bar height at the bottom. Dual Orientation The isVertical property is bound to the persisted setting (Display \u2192 Layout \u2192 \"Nav Bar Position\", a SettingsSegmentedControl with the options \"bottom\" and \"side\" ): property bool isVertical: settingsManager && settingsManager.bottomBarOrientation === \"side\" updateLayout() re-anchors the bar. Anchors are released (set to undefined ) before the new ones are assigned, which avoids a transient state where conflicting anchors are active at the same time: function updateLayout () { if (isVertical) { anchors.bottom = undefined anchors.right = undefined ancho"}, {"url": "building.html", "title": "Running from Source", "subtitle": "How the Python backend is run: setup.py, the venv, requirements.txt, per-platform notes, the phone-mirror helpers, ruff and pytest, and the GitHub Actions lint / test jobs. There is no build or packaging step.", "headings": [{"id": "quick-start", "text": "Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "requirements", "text": "Requirements &amp; Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "python", "text": "What setup.py Does", "level": 2}, {"id": "running", "text": "Running main.py", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux", "text": "Linux &amp; Raspberry Pi", "level": 2}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows", "level": 2}, {"id": "macos", "text": "macOS", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-server", "text": "Phone Server Jar &amp; adb", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-files", "text": "Developer-Local Files", "level": 2}, {"id": "lint-tests", "text": "Lint &amp; Tests", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci-cd", "text": "GitHub Actions CI", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "text": "Setup Troubleshooting", "level": 2}], "body": "Running from Source - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Running from Source Running from Source How the Python backend is run: setup.py , the venv, requirements.txt , per-platform notes, the phone-mirror helpers, ruff and pytest, and the GitHub Actions lint / test jobs. There is no build or packaging step. Quick Start The Python backend ( backend/ + main.py ) runs straight from a checkout. Nothing is compiled and nothing is packaged: there is no installer, no PyInstaller spec and no wheel. python setup.py # create ./venv, install requirements.txt, launch main.py (installs distro packages on Linux via sudo) python setup.py --no-run # same setup, print the run instructions, don't launch # Afterwards source venv/bin/activate # Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate python main.py # normal run python main.py --debug # DEBUG logging + octave-debug.log Without setup.py the equivalent is a venv of your own plus pip install -r requirements.txt , then python main.py . Requirements & Dependencies Python 3.10 or newer. pyproject.toml declares requires-python = \">=3.10\" with no upper cap, and setup.py exits with Python 3.10+ required below that. CI runs Python 3.11. A display. main.py creates a QApplication and loads frontend/Main.qml ; headless runs need QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen (as the tests use). Platforms: Linux (including Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi), Windows and macOS. The backend is desktop-only; there is no Android or iOS path. requirements.txt (what setup.py and CI install): Package Pin Used by PySide6 >=6.8.0 Qt bindings: QML engine, Qt Multimedia, Qt Quick 3D and the other Qt modules the app imports mutagen ==1.47.0 Tag and cover-art reading ( MediaManager ) numpy >=1.26.0 FFT ( AudioAnalyzer ); phone-mirror audio-level and frame checks; imported lazily obd (python-obd) >=0.7.3 ELM327 I/O ( OBDManager ); imported lazily av (PyAV) >=12.0.0 Audio decode for the visualizer, H.264 decode for phone mirroring requests >=2.25.0 HTTP inside backend/music_dl (cover art, metadata) pyserial >=3.5 ESP32 knob, OBD serial ports smbus2 >=0.4.3 , only when sys_platform == \"linux\" I\u00b2C for the BerryIMU and gesture sensor yt-dlp ==2026.8.19 Downloads (vendored backend/music_dl ) ytmusicapi ==1.11.5 YouTube Music search rapidfuzz >=3.0.0 Result matching in music_dl pyproject.toml carries a shorter dependencies list (PySide6, mutagen, requests, yt-dlp >=2026.8.19 , ytmusicapi, rapidfuzz), the version 0.9.5 and the ruff configuration; nothing installs the project from it. Not in requirements.txt : pytest and ruff (install them in the venv for development) and psutil (optional, used only by the developer-local performance monitor). Optional Qt modules are probed at runtime rather than required: main.py sets wikiViewerAvailable only if PySide6.QtWebEngineQuick imports, and the 3D vehicle view greys itself out when Qt Quick 3D cannot be loaded (see Architecture ). What setup.py Does Console encoding \u2014 if stdout is not UTF-8 (cp1252 on Windows) it reconfigures stdout/stderr to UTF-8 with errors=\"replace\" so the \u2713 / \u2717 / \u2192 glyphs print. chdir into the checkout, then detect the platform : macos , windows , raspberry (Linux whose /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model contains \"raspberry\") or linux , plus WSL ( microsoft in /proc/version ). Python version check \u2014 exit 1 below 3.10. System packages (Linux and Raspberry Pi only) \u2014 detects the distro family from /etc/os-release (falling back to whichever of pacman / apt / dnf / yum exists) and installs the Qt runtime libraries with sudo: pacman -S --needed --noconfirm on Arch / Manjaro / EndeavourOS, dnf install -y on Fedora / RHEL / CentOS / Rocky, and apt update + apt install -y on Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS and anything unrecognised. The install command's exit status is ignored, so a package missing on your release does not stop setup. macOS and Windows install nothing here. venv at <checkout>/venv . An existing one is kept only if pyvenv.cfg exists, was written on the same OS family (it looks for C:\\ in the file to tell a Windows venv from a Linux/macOS one) and its pip --version runs. Otherwise it is removed with shutil.rmtree(..., onexc=...) ; if removal fails the folder is renamed to venv_old_<timestamp> . A new venv is created with venv.create(..., with_pip=True) ; on failure it suggests python3-venv (Debian) or python (Arch). Python packages \u2014 pip install --upgrade pip , then pip install -r requirements.txt ; a failure there exits 1. Launch \u2014 unless --no-run was passed, os.execv s the venv's Python on main.py with no extra arguments. Under WSL with neither DISPLAY nor WAYLAND_DISPLAY set it prints display guidance and skips the launch. onexc needs Python 3.12 The onexc= keyword of shutil.rmtree was added in Python 3.12. On 3.10 and 3.11 the call raises TypeError , which setup.py catches like any other removal failure, so an invalid venv is renamed to venv_old_<timestamp> instead of deleted. Remove those folders by hand. Running main.py main.py parses its flags with argparse.parse_known_args , so "}, {"url": "components.html", "title": "Components", "subtitle": "Reusable top-level QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, ThemedIcon, TerminalFeedback, CornerBrackets, GalleryTile, SensorCard, GForceDisplay, HomeOBDView, OBDAdapterSheet, the OBDParameterModel singleton and the rigged Jeep TJ", "headings": [{"id": "scope", "text": "Scope and Conventions", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-carousel", "text": "AlbumArtCarousel", "level": 2}, {"id": "coverflow-card", "text": "CoverflowCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "themed-icon", "text": "ThemedIcon", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-devices", "text": "Music Device Components", "level": 2}, {"id": "terminal-feedback", "text": "TerminalFeedback", "level": 2}, {"id": "corner-brackets", "text": "CornerBrackets", "level": 2}, {"id": "gallery-tile", "text": "GalleryTile", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensor-card", "text": "SensorCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "gforce-display", "text": "GForceDisplay", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-qr-pairing-panel", "text": "PhoneQrPairingPanel", "level": 2}, {"id": "home-obd-view", "text": "HomeOBDView", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-adapter-sheet", "text": "OBDAdapterSheet", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-parameter-model", "text": "OBDParameterModel", "level": 2}, {"id": "jeep-rig", "text": "The Jeep TJ Rig", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Components - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Components Components Reusable top-level QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, ThemedIcon, TerminalFeedback, CornerBrackets, GalleryTile, SensorCard, GForceDisplay, HomeOBDView, OBDAdapterSheet, the OBDParameterModel singleton and the rigged Jeep TJ Scope and Conventions This page covers the reusable QML types that live at the top of frontend/ . The settings component library ( settings/ : cards, tiles, toggles, sliders, chips, hub widgets) is documented in Settings UI ; the gauge primitives ( gauges/ ) and the dashboard widgets ( dashboards/widgets/ ) in Gauges & Dashboards ; the design-token singletons ( Style , Spacing , EnvironmentTheme ) in Theme System . Full views are on the Pages page. Because frontend/ is on the QML import path, every file here is usable by its type name without an import. Files that import \".\" as App reach the singletons as App.Style / App.Spacing / App.EnvironmentTheme . Almost every component carries the same two-line local dp() / dpMin() wrapper (see GUI Scale for why). Component Root type Instantiated by AlbumArtCarousel Item MediaRoom.qml (and, through it, the Now Playing Studio preview) CoverflowCard Item AlbumArtCarousel (three instances) ThemedIcon Item settings tiles and cards, hub widgets TerminalFeedback Rectangle settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml (library-scan output); OBDDiagnostics.qml has its own inline terminal instead CornerBrackets Item TerminalFeedback , SettingsCard , SettingsTile , SettingsDashboardCard , SettingsHubCard , SettingsHubGridCard (always hidden, see below) GalleryTile Rectangle the Primitives Gallery popup in OBDMenu.qml SensorCard , GForceDisplay Rectangle SensorMenu.qml HomeOBDView Item MainMenu.qml OBDAdapterSheet Popup OBDMenu.qml (empty-state Connect button), settings/OBDSettingsPage.qml (Adapters card) OBDParameterModel QtObject singleton every OBD view, the gauges, the dashboard editor, Main.qml's IMU gating JeepVehicle , JeepHinge , JeepLamp , JeepLink Quick3D Node CarMenu.qml AlbumArtCarousel frontend/AlbumArtCarousel.qml is the album-art display at the centre of MediaRoom . It owns three CoverflowCard s (previous, centre, next), a frozen \"exit snapshot\" of the outgoing art, the dissolve shader, and ten selectable track-change animations. The header comment states the design: one unified transition list \u2014 transitionStyle picks any of the ten names, and each animation reads previewEnabled to decide whether the side cards take part (3D-flavoured animations animate them when the preview is on and no-op that part when it is off; 2D-flavoured animations never touch them). Public API // Inputs property int direction: 1 // +1 next, -1 previous property url artSource: \"\" property url prevArtSource: \"\" property url nextArtSource: \"\" property string transitionStyle: \"Crossfade\" property bool previewEnabled: false // show 3D side cards property bool roundedArt: false property real artRadius: 0 property bool vinylMode: false property real sideCardAngle: 30 property real sideCardOpacity: 0.4 property bool showShadow: false property real playbackPosition: 0 // vinyl rotation property real playbackDuration: 0 // Outputs property bool animBusy: false readonly property bool dissolveActive readonly property real dissolveProgress readonly property url dissolveNewArt property real artSize // min(w,h) * (previewEnabled ? 0.75 : 0.85) signal animationFinished () signal settled () // 50 ms after animationFinished, animBusy is false again signal artClicked () signal artLoadFailed () function triggerSlide () // dispatch on transitionStyle function showSideCards () // re-bind side-card opacity after a 3D animation MediaRoom binds the inputs straight to settings: show3DAlbumPreview , roundedAlbumArt , albumArtCornerRadius (through dp() ), vinylRecordMode , sideCardAngle , sideCardOpacity , showAlbumArtShadow and albumArtTransition . There is no separate \"3D style\" setting; the same albumArtTransition string selects a 2D or 3D animation. Transition styles and constants Style Flavour Constants (top of file) Notes \"Crossfade\" (default and fallback) 2D kCrossfadeDuration 250 , kCrossfadeScale 0.97 Old art fades out while the new art scales up from 0.97 \"Slide\" 2D kSlideDuration 280 , kSlideDistance 80 (passed through dp() ) Direction-aware horizontal slide \"Vinyl Lift\" 2D kLiftDuration 180 , kLiftPause 60 , kLiftScale 0.85 Sequential: lift out, pause, drop in with OutBack overshoot 1.2 \u2014 420 ms in total \"Dissolve\" 2D kDissolveDuration 900 , kDissolveGridSize 20 , kDissolveSmoothness 0.1 GPU shader, see below \"Flip\" 2D kFlipDuration 350 (half per side) Y-axis flip: exit snapshot turns away, new art turns in \"Coverflow\" , \"Conveyor\" , \"Stack\" , \"Depth\" , \"Swing\" 3D k3dDuration 350 , k3dSideOffsetFactor 0.42 , k3dSideScale 0.72 , k3dFarScale 0.5 Side cards move into the centre slot; a few per-style numbers are inline (Coverflow fast-fade 220 ms, Swing \u00b170\u00b0 exit / \u00b160\u00b0 side, Depth 0.5 exit / 0.4 far scale) Every animation sets"}, {"url": "development.html", "title": "Development Guide", "subtitle": "Working on the Python backend: dev mode and the mock OBD, the command server and MCP servers, the perf monitor, screenshots and video stories, adding a feature, the pytest suite, ruff and CI \u2014 and why the tooling is built this way.", "headings": [{"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "repo-map", "text": "Repository Map for Developers", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Dev Mode Setup &amp; Usage", "level": 2}, {"id": "mock-obd", "text": "Mock OBD Manager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tools", "text": "DevToolsPanel", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command Server (TCP)", "level": 2}, {"id": "mcp-server", "text": "MCP Servers", "level": 2}, {"id": "profiling", "text": "Performance Profiling", "level": 2}, {"id": "screenshots", "text": "Screenshots &amp; Video Stories", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-features", "text": "How to Add a Feature", "level": 2}, {"id": "testing", "text": "Testing &amp; Linting", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci", "text": "Continuous Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "todo", "text": "Deferred Work: the TODO Folder", "level": 2}], "body": "Development Guide - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Development Guide Development Guide Working on the Python backend: dev mode and the mock OBD, the command server and MCP servers, the perf monitor, screenshots and video stories, adding a feature, the pytest suite, ruff and CI \u2014 and why the tooling is built this way. Design Decisions Why dev mode wraps production instead of duplicating it python -m dev.main_dev imports main , which runs the whole production start-up at import time (app, engine, every manager, every signal connection) and stops short of loading the QML, because load_main_qml() and app.exec() only run under main.py 's if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard. Dev mode then swaps in the mock OBD through main.install_obd_manager() , registers its context properties, calls main.load_main_qml() and reparents an overlay into the real window. The docstring of dev/main_dev.py records why: the old dev/dev_main.py \"duplicated 85% of main.py and constantly drifted out of parity\". Everything except obdManager is the production object. Why a TCP command server and MCP servers for performance work The head unit runs in a Jeep; the problems that matter are stutter, stalls and regressions that show up when the app is driven the way a driver drives it. A JSON-over-TCP command server ( backend/command_server.py , only started by --profile ) lets a script or an agent navigate pages, toggle settings, skip tracks, drive the simulator and read the perf counters while the perf monitor measures the event loop, so a test is repeatable and comparable between changes. The two MCP servers expose the same channel to a coding agent so it can reproduce a report, bisect a regression or benchmark a setting without a human clicking. Where the tooling lives The developer tooling is not in git : dev/ (dev runtime, mock OBD, screenshot / story / collage / perf scripts, MCP servers) and the three in-app modules backend/command_server.py , backend/perf_monitor.py and backend/perf_patches.py are listed in .gitignore and synced between the developer machines outside git. A fresh clone does not have them. main.py imports the three modules only inside setup_perf_profiling() (run for --profile ) and guards each import: a missing module logs a warning and the app keeps starting \u2014 see Troubleshooting . The dev overlay QML that dev/dev_runtime.py loads ( DevOverlay.qml , which embeds DevToolsPanel.qml ) is read from src/dev/qml/ . The launchers in dev/ do not start the Python app unless told to; every use below therefore either runs python -m dev.main_dev / python main.py --profile directly or sets OCTAVE_BACKEND=python (or passes --backend python where a script has that flag). Why screenshots are grabbed from inside Qt The first screenshot tool ( dev/screenshots/core.py , scenarios.py , cli.py ) used xdotool + mss and broke under Wayland. The current one asks the running app to capture itself: the screenshot and record_start actions call grabWindow() on the GUI thread, which works the same on Wayland, X11 and Xvfb, and the HUD can be hidden first with set_dev_chrome . It also gives deterministic window sizes for README assets ( dev/docs/SCREENSHOTS.md ). Why ruff runs warn-only The full ruleset ( E F W E722 B UP ) is enabled in pyproject.toml , but the CI step is ruff check . || true , labelled \"warn-only \u2014 full ruleset has legacy noise\". A second CI step, ruff check . --select E722,F821 , is blocking: bare except: and undefined names fail the build. Why errors do not surface in the UI Errors are reported through logs and page-specific status text. There is no application-wide error notification layer; TODO/error-notification-ui.md records that deferred design. Individual views can show error details on request (tapping a failed download opens its error popup). The rotating logs, the UI watchdog ( backend/ui_watchdog.py ) and the Diagnostics card ( backend/diagnostics_manager.py ) support troubleshooting after a session. Repository Map for Developers Path Tracked? What it is main.py , setup.py yes Entry point (flags --debug , --profile , --smoke ) and the one-shot installer backend/ yes The managers, one module each, plus helpers: logging_config.py , atomic_io.py , lazy_import.py , qt_threading.py ( run_on_main ), ui_watchdog.py , volume_utils.py , version.py , elm327_protocol.py ; packages android_auto/ , phone_mirror/ , obd_transports/ , music_dl/ frontend/ yes The QML UI, gauges/ , dashboards/ (renderer, catalog, presets/*.json , editor/ , widgets/ ), settings/ , shaders/ , assets/ tests/ yes The pytest suite ( Testing ), fake_scrcpy_server.py and QML fixtures/ phone_server/ , tools/phone-server/ yes Forked scrcpy server source and the prebuilt jar that the phone-mirror client pushes over adb docs/ yes GAUGE_AUTHORING.md (the gauge/dashboard spec), PHONE_MIRROR_NATIVE_PLAN.md (protocol byte layouts), vehicle/ references tools/vehicle/ , tools/music_devices/ yes Generators for the Jeep model and the music-device GLBs in frontend/assets/ TODO/ yes D"}, {"url": "download-manager.html", "title": "Download Manager", "subtitle": "YouTube Music search (ytmusicapi) and yt-dlp downloads through the vendored music_dl engine, with ffmpeg conversion and mutagen tagging", "headings": [], "body": "Download Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Download Manager Download Manager YouTube Music search (ytmusicapi) and yt-dlp downloads through the vendored music_dl engine, with ffmpeg conversion and mutagen tagging Overview The DownloadManager ( backend/download_manager.py ) lets the user search YouTube Music from the head unit and save songs into the local media library, where MediaManager picks them up. It is registered in main.py as the QML context property downloadManager and driven by frontend/DownloadPage.qml . The work is done by the vendored music_dl engine ( backend/music_dl/ , a trimmed fork of spotdl). It searches with ytmusicapi , downloads with yt-dlp used as a Python library, launches the ffmpeg executable for conversion and writes tags and cover art with mutagen . YouTube Only, No Credentials Search uses the anonymous ytmusicapi client and needs no account or API key. Downloads may need a cookies file when YouTube rejects an unauthenticated request (see Bot Wall ). System Architecture DownloadManager (backend/download_manager.py) | |-- MusicDL engine (lazy init on first search/download) | |-- Downloader (download/downloader.py) | | |-- providers/audio/base.py yt-dlp YoutubeDL (library call) | | |-- providers/audio/ytmusic.py YouTubeMusic (ytmusicapi, used when a song has no URL) | | |-- utils/ffmpeg.py conversion (ffmpeg subprocess) | | |-- utils/metadata.py mutagen tags + cover embedding | | |-- download/progress_handler.py callback -> _on_download_progress() | |-- utils/search.py get_ytm_client() used by DownloadManager search | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (2 workers) search + downloads |-- QTimer _poll_timer (200 ms) drains progress list, resolves futures |-- _progress_updates + _progress_lock Every slot runs on the GUI thread and hands the blocking work to the two-worker executor. Futures are kept in _pending_futures under the key \"__search__\" or \"dl_<counter>_<song_id>\" . The 200 ms timer runs only while a future or a progress update is pending. The music_dl Engine backend/music_dl/ is OCTAVE's fork of spotdl ( __version__ = \"4.4.3\" in _version.py ; MIT licence, copyright 2021 spotDL Developers, kept in backend/music_dl/LICENSE ). The fork strips the CLI, the web UI, SponsorBlock, lyrics providers, the non-YouTube sources and the Rich terminal progress bars: download/progress_handler.py is a \"callback-based progress handler\" that invokes a function instead of drawing a TUI, and the only audio provider left is providers/audio/ytmusic.py ( DOWNLOADER_OPTIONS[\"audio_providers\"] = [\"youtube-music\"] in utils/config.py ). Entry Point: MusicDL Class class MusicDL : def __init__ ( self , downloader_settings= None ): self .downloader = Downloader (settings=downloader_settings) def get_download_urls ( self , songs): # YouTube Music URL per song (multi-threaded) def download ( self , song): # -> Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]] def download_songs ( self , songs): # -> List[Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]]] def cleanup ( self ): # nothing to release; kept for the shutdown path DownloadManager only calls download() and cleanup() . Settings passed by DownloadManager _ensure_music_dl() builds the engine on first use with these downloader_settings (every other key is filled from DOWNLOADER_OPTIONS in utils/config.py ): downloader_settings = { \"output\" : os.path.join(download_path, \"{artists} - {title}.{output-ext}\" ), \"format\" : dl_format, # downloadFormat setting, default \"mp3\" \"bitrate\" : bitrate, # downloadBitrate setting, default \"auto\" \"threads\" : 2 , \"overwrite\" : \"skip\" , \"_progress_callback\" : self ._on_download_progress, } if cookies_path: downloader_settings[ \"cookie_file\" ] = cookies_path The Downloader constructor reads _progress_callback before the settings are filtered to the known option keys and installs it as the progress handler's update_callback . The constructor also locates ffmpeg: ffmpeg on PATH , else an ffmpeg binary in the music_dl data directory. When neither exists it raises DownloaderError(\"ffmpeg is not installed\") ; _ensure_music_dl() catches that, emits statusMessage(\"Download engine error: ffmpeg is not installed\") and leaves the engine unset, so search is unavailable too until ffmpeg is installed. A successful build emits \"Download engine ready\". Working directories get_music_dl_path() in utils/config.py is ~/.config/music_dl on Linux, ~/Library/Application Support/music_dl on macOS and ~/.music_dl elsewhere (Windows). yt-dlp downloads into its temp/ subfolder as <videoId>.<ext> ; a failed ffmpeg conversion writes its command and output to errors/ffmpeg_error_<timestamp>.txt . Sub-Module Reference Module Purpose download/downloader.py Core download + convert + tag loop; uses song.download_url (or searches the providers for one), builds a per-song AudioProvider , adds the yt-dlp progress hook, converts with ffmpeg unless the source can be kept, embeds metadata into a staging file and publishes it ( partial_path_for() / publish_partial() ) download/progress_handler.py ProgressHandler / S"}, {"url": "frontend-overview.html", "title": "Frontend Overview", "subtitle": "QML architecture, the file inventory under frontend/, Main.qml structure, page routing, scaling, effects, shaders, and the bridge between the Python backend (main.py, backend/) and the UI", "headings": [{"id": "architecture", "text": "QML Architecture Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "file-inventory", "text": "File Inventory", "level": 2}, {"id": "main-qml", "text": "Main.qml Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "window-management", "text": "Window Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-routing", "text": "Page Routing Mechanism", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-cache", "text": "Lazy loading and the page cache", "level": 3}, {"id": "imu-gating", "text": "IMU Rate Gating from the StackView", "level": 2}, {"id": "import-path", "text": "Import Path and Singletons", "level": 2}, {"id": "controls-style", "text": "Qt Quick Controls \"Basic\" Style", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Accessing Backend Managers (Context Properties)", "level": 2}, {"id": "typeof-guards", "text": "Guarding Optional Globals with typeof", "level": 2}, {"id": "gui-scale", "text": "GUI Scale", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-loading", "text": "Dynamic Font Loading", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "shift-light", "text": "Shift Light Overlays", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-connections", "text": "Settings Manager Connections", "level": 2}, {"id": "drop-shadows", "text": "Drop Shadows", "level": 2}, {"id": "graphical-effects", "text": "Graphical Effects: Qt5Compat vs MultiEffect", "level": 2}, {"id": "shaders", "text": "Shaders", "level": 2}, {"id": "objectnames", "text": "objectName Conventions for Dev Scripting", "level": 2}, {"id": "keyboard", "text": "Keyboard Handling", "level": 2}, {"id": "initialization", "text": "Initialization Sequence", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Frontend Overview - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Frontend Overview Frontend Overview QML architecture, the file inventory under frontend/ , Main.qml structure, page routing, scaling, effects, shaders, and the bridge between the Python backend ( main.py , backend/ ) and the UI QML Architecture Overview OCTAVE's frontend uses Qt Quick / QML. Most files import QtQuick 2.15 ; the optional vehicle and music-device scenes use unversioned Qt 6 imports and QtQuick3D . The Python managers in backend/ expose playback, hardware and persistence through context properties registered in main.py , while QML implements presentation and interaction. main.py loads the tree straight from disk; there is no QML compilation or packaging step. The media components use unversioned imports and set the revisioned layer.live property through Binding { target: item.layer; property: \"live\"; value: ... } : their source comments document order-dependent Qt 6.7 failures with a direct layer.live: declaration. ApplicationWindow (Main.qml) +--------------------------------------------------------------+ | mainContainer (Item, anchors.fill) | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | StackView id: stackView objectName: \"stackView\" z:1 | | | | initialItem: MainMenu | | | | pushed lazily: MediaRoom, MediaPlayer, DownloadPage, | | | | OBDHome, OBDMenu, OBDDiagnostics, SensorHome, | | | | SensorMenu, CarMenu, ClockMenu, SettingsMenu, | | | | PhoneMirrorView, AndroidAutoView, | | | | dashboards/editor/DashboardEditor | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | bottomBarShadow / sideBarShadow (gradient strips) z:2 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | BottomBar (persistent nav + transport + volume) z:0 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | NowPlayingStudio (window-level overlay) z:5 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | globalFullScreenFlash (Rectangle) z:999999 | | | | globalAuraFlash (4 edge gradients) z:999999 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | +--------------------------------------------------------------+ | FolderListModel (assets/fonts) + dynamic FontLoaders | | Timers: navReloadTimer (150 ms), globalFlashTimer | +--------------------------------------------------------------+ Key architectural points: Single window \u2014 one ApplicationWindow hosts everything, including full-window overlays (Now Playing Studio, shift-light flash). QML popups and dialogs stay in that window; native file pickers and the Android Auto DHU may use platform windows. StackView navigation \u2014 pages are pushed/popped on a StackView ; there is no router. Most navigation pages are instantiated on first visit or during prewarming and then cached ( stackView.openPage() , see the page cache ), so later visits re-push the same instance. Persistent BottomBar \u2014 always visible below (horizontal) or to the left of (vertical \"side\" mode) the StackView. See Bottom Bar . Singletons declared in qmldir \u2014 Style , Spacing , EnvironmentTheme , OBDParameterModel , dashboards/WidgetCatalog , and MusicDevicePreference are pragma Singleton objects. No page transitions \u2014 all six StackView transitions ( pushEnter/Exit , popEnter/Exit , replaceEnter/Exit ) are null . No global z-order tricks beyond the ones in the diagram: the StackView sits above the BottomBar ( z: 1 vs 0 ), shadows at z: 2 , the studio at z: 5 , overlays at z: 999999 . Above them sits inputActivity ( z: 1000000 ), an empty item whose PointHandler takes only a passive grab: it records the last press so page pre-building can stay out of the way of taps, and never accepts the event. File Inventory Everything the UI needs lives under frontend/ . The top level is flat; only settings, gauges, dashboards, shaders and assets get subdirectories. File Role Documented in Main.qml ApplicationWindow, StackView, overlays, font scan, shift light, navigation helpers this page qmldir Registers the six singletons (see Import Path ) this page Style.qml , Spacing.qml , EnvironmentTheme.qml Design-token singletons: colors/fonts/themes, scaled dimensions, structural tokens Theme System , GUI Scale BottomBar.qml Persistent navigation, transport, volume Bottom Bar MainMenu.qml , HomeOBDView.qml Home screen and its OBD card grid Pages , Components MediaRoom.qml , MediaPlayer.qml , DownloadPage.qml Now-playing view, library browser, YouTube Music search/download Pages MusicDevicePreference.qml , MusicDeviceScene.qml , MusicDeviceCover.qml , MusicDeviceArtSurface.qml Playback-device preference singleton and optional Quick3D music-device visuals Components NowPlayingStudio.qml Window-level immersive editor for Now Playing visuals Pages AlbumArtCarousel.qml , CoverflowCard.qml , ThemedIcon.qml , GalleryTile.qml , CornerBrackets.qml , TerminalFeedback.qml Reusable components Components OBDHome.qml , OBDMenu.qml , OBDDiagnostics.qml , OBDParameterModel.qml OBD hub, live gauges/dash"}, {"url": "gauges-dashboards.html", "title": "Gauges &amp; Dashboards", "subtitle": "Reusable gauge primitives, the OBDParameterModel binding layer, schema-1 JSON dashboards rendered by DashboardRenderer, DashboardManager (backend/dashboard_manager.py), and the in-app drag-and-tap dashboard editor", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "files", "text": "Files at a Glance", "level": 2}, {"id": "parameter-model", "text": "Data Flow: OBDParameterModel", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-gate", "text": "Vehicle Gate and Polling Demand", "level": 2}, {"id": "binding-api", "text": "Shared Binding API", "level": 2}, {"id": "primitives", "text": "Gauge Primitives", "level": 2}, {"id": "self-binding", "text": "Self-Binding Widgets", "level": 2}, {"id": "angle-math", "text": "Angle and Needle Math", "level": 2}, {"id": "schema", "text": "Dashboard JSON Schema (schema 1)", "level": 2}, {"id": "presets", "text": "Built-in Presets", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboard-manager", "text": "DashboardManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "sharing", "text": "Sharing: Export and Import", "level": 2}, {"id": "renderer", "text": "DashboardRenderer: Spec to QML", "level": 2}, {"id": "widget-catalog", "text": "WidgetCatalog", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdmenu", "text": "OBDMenu Integration: Active Dashboard and Chooser", "level": 2}, {"id": "editor", "text": "Dashboard Editor Internals", "level": 2}, {"id": "validation", "text": "Validation and Hot Reload", "level": 2}, {"id": "testing", "text": "Testing", "level": 2}, {"id": "authoring", "text": "Authoring New Gauges and Dashboards", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Gauges & Dashboards - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Gauges & Dashboards Gauges & Dashboards Reusable gauge primitives, the OBDParameterModel binding layer, schema-1 JSON dashboards rendered by DashboardRenderer, DashboardManager ( backend/dashboard_manager.py ), and the in-app drag-and-tap dashboard editor Overview OCTAVE's OBD view has two presentations: the built-in Parameter Cards grid inside frontend/OBDMenu.qml , and dashboards -- JSON specs that place gauge primitives on a grid. Five presets ship in frontend/dashboards/presets/ ; users build their own in an in-app editor, or drop a JSON file into the user dashboards directory. DashboardManager ( backend/dashboard_manager.py , context property dashboardManager ) enumerates both sources, watches the user directory, and exposes save / delete / duplicate plus export / import for sharing. frontend/dashboards/DashboardRenderer.qml turns a spec into live widgets; the chooser popup shows every dashboard as a scaled live miniature; the selection persists as the activeDashboard setting, and a horizontal swipe on the OBD page cycles through Parameter Cards and every dashboard. Parameter Cards lists every PID the connected vehicle supports, and a vehicle gate keeps dashboards from showing PIDs that vehicle doesn't report. Every gauge binds to the OBDParameterModel singleton by paramId ( \"RPM\" , \"SPEED\" , \"LATERAL_G\" ...) -- title, unit, min and max are auto-populated from the model and the live value updates reactively. No gauge touches obdManager directly, so the same gauges run against the real OBD manager ( backend/obd_manager.py ), the dev-mode mock ( dev/mock_obd_manager.py , installed by python -m dev.main_dev ), or the editor's demo data. The step-by-step authoring spec is docs/GAUGE_AUTHORING.md ; this page documents how the pieces are built and why. Where the two disagree, the code wins and both should be updated. Files at a Glance frontend/ OBDParameterModel.qml singleton: allParameters (129 ids + kinds), paramValues, simulation, vehicle gate OBDMenu.qml Parameter Cards (vehicle PIDs), DashboardRenderer host, swipe, chooser (Import/Share), gallery qmldir registers OBDParameterModel and WidgetCatalog as singletons gauges/ CircularGauge.qml arc + ticks + optional needle + centre readout ArcGauge.qml top half-dial with readout below the chord BarGauge.qml horizontal / vertical filled bar with warn threshold LinearGauge.qml scale with bidirectional fill from zero DigitalReadout.qml big number + title + unit SparklineGauge.qml timer-sampled rolling history line (Canvas) WarningLight.qml threshold tell-tale GForceGauge.qml self-binding LATERAL_G / LONGITUDINAL_G dot + trail CompassGauge.qml self-binding HEADING compass card dashboards/ DashboardRenderer.qml spec -> absolutely positioned Loaders WidgetCatalog.qml singleton: type -> url, palette meta, supportedKinds, label/range/color flags, editableProps, colorSwatches presets/ sport, minimal, fullgrid, performance, tj-wrangler (.json) widgets/ NowPlayingWidget.qml self-binding media card MediaControlsWidget.qml self-binding transport buttons editor/ DashboardEditor.qml page: owns the working spec, save / cancel / draft / demo EditorCanvas.qml [+] targets, selection, drag-to-move, corner resize grip, occupancy map PalettePopup.qml widget palette (reads WidgetCatalog) PIDPicker.qml searchable, kind-filtered, vehicle-gated PID list PropertiesPanel.qml PID / position & size / options / label & range / color inspector backend/dashboard_manager.py DashboardManager (context property dashboardManager) docs/GAUGE_AUTHORING.md authoring spec (source of truth for the API) dev/tests/drive_dashboard_editor.py end-to-end editor scenario (command server) dev/tests/drive_dashboard_features.py overrides, resize, swipe, sharing scenario (command server) tests/test_dashboard_sharing.py import sanitizer + export/import round trip (pytest) tests/test_vehicle_gate_qml.py headless QML gate test with a fake obdManager (pytest) Data Flow: OBDParameterModel obdManager / berryIMU -- *Changed(float) --> OBDParameterModel -- paramValues[id] --> gauge bindings (backend/*.py) (dedicated + generic handlers) _pendingValues _normalized 33 ms flush timer _animNorm (Behavior) frontend/OBDParameterModel.qml (registered as a singleton in frontend/qmldir ) declares: Member Description allParameters Array of { id, title, unit, min, max, kind } : 129 entries: 121 OBD entries (including the IGNITION_TIMING alias and adapter voltage) and 8 BerryIMU readings. Of the OBD entries, 37 newer values arrive through obdParameterChanged(paramId, value) . kind One of percentage (0-100 %), temperature (\u00b0C), voltage , pressure (kPa/Pa), bidirectional (signed, straddles zero: trims, timing, EGR error, WR currents, pitch/roll/G), numeric (everything else). Widgets declare which kinds they accept; the PID picker filters on it. originalParameters , isOriginalParameter(id) The historical group of 18 ids, also used as the settings list fallback before a supported-PID list "}, {"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting Started", "subtitle": "Installing and running the Python / PySide6 backend from a checkout: setup.py, the virtual environment,\n            per-platform notes, command-line flags, dev mode, and the directory layout.", "headings": [{"id": "requirements", "text": "System Requirements", "level": 2}, {"id": "quick-start", "text": "Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "setup-script", "text": "What setup.py Does", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-linux", "text": "Linux", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux-arch", "text": "Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-debian", "text": "Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-fedora", "text": "Fedora / RHEL / CentOS / Rocky", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-rpi", "text": "Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi", "level": 3}, {"id": "platform-windows", "text": "Windows", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-macos", "text": "macOS", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-wsl", "text": "WSL Notes", "level": 2}, {"id": "running", "text": "Running OCTAVE", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Developer Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Python Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "directory-structure", "text": "Directory Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "first-steps", "text": "First Steps After Install", "level": 2}], "body": "Getting Started - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Getting Started Getting Started Installing and running the Python / PySide6 backend from a checkout: setup.py, the virtual environment, per-platform notes, command-line flags, dev mode, and the directory layout. System Requirements Requirement Minimum Notes OS Linux, Windows, macOS Desktop only. setup.py recognises Raspberry Pi from the device-tree model string; other Debian-based boards (e.g. Orange Pi) take the normal Linux path. Python 3.10+ pyproject.toml declares requires-python = \">=3.10\" with no upper bound and setup.py exits below 3.10; CI tests Python 3.11. Qt PySide6 6.8.0+ Installed from PyPI by requirements.txt ; the wheel brings its own Qt libraries. Display Any size main.py divides the shorter edge of the primary screen's available size by 720 and clamps the result to 0.4\u20133.0 ( screenAutoScale ); the user's UI scale setting is applied on top. Without a primary screen it uses 1.0. Audio Working system audio output Playback uses Qt Multimedia from PySide6; available codecs depend on that build. Network Optional Needed for music search / download and update checks. Local playback, OBD and USB phone mirroring work offline. Quick Start From zero to a running app: # Clone the repository git clone https://github.com/RobDeGeorge/OCTAVE.git cd OCTAVE # Setup and run (creates venv, installs deps, launches app) python setup.py To install without launching: python setup.py --no-run To run manually after setup: # Linux / macOS source venv/bin/activate python main.py # Windows venv\\Scripts\\activate python main.py Runs from a checkout The Python backend is not packaged: there is no installer or frozen executable. It always runs as python main.py inside the checkout's virtual environment, and it reads the QML from frontend/ on disk, so a QML change shows on the next launch. What setup.py Does setup.py The script changes to its own directory and performs these steps in order: Detects your platform \u2014 Windows, macOS, Linux, or Raspberry Pi (if /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model contains \"raspberry\"), and WSL (\"microsoft\" in /proc/version ). Checks the Python version \u2014 exits with \"Python 3.10+ required\" below 3.10. Installs system dependencies (Linux and Raspberry Pi only, via sudo ) \u2014 the distro family (Arch, Debian, Fedora) comes from /etc/os-release , falling back to whichever of pacman , apt , dnf / yum is on PATH ; anything unrecognised takes the Debian list. The packages are audio (PulseAudio/ALSA), OpenGL/EGL, the XCB platform-plugin libraries, X11 and input, fonts, D-Bus, and on Arch/Fedora the Qt 6 base + multimedia packages. Package-manager failures do not stop the script, so read its output if something is still missing. On macOS and Windows this step only prints a note. Creates a virtual environment at ./venv/ with the built-in venv module. An existing venv is kept if its pyvenv.cfg matches the OS family (a C:\\ path marks a Windows venv) and its pip --version runs; otherwise it is deleted and recreated. If the deletion fails it is renamed to venv_old_<timestamp> (the deletion passes shutil.rmtree(onexc=...) , which needs Python 3.12, so on 3.10 and 3.11 the rename is what happens). Installs Python dependencies \u2014 upgrades pip , then pip install -r requirements.txt ; a failure exits the script. Launches the application \u2014 unless --no-run is passed (it then prints the activate-and-run commands) or WSL has no display, it replaces itself with the venv's python main.py via os.execv() . Linux Linux is the main development platform. The setup script installs the system-level dependencies below through your distro's package manager; they are what PySide6's xcb platform plugin and Qt Multimedia need at runtime. Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS System packages installed via pacman : sudo pacman -S --needed --noconfirm python python-pip libpulse alsa-lib mesa libglvnd \\ libxcb xcb-util xcb-util-cursor xcb-util-image xcb-util-keysyms \\ xcb-util-renderutil xcb-util-wm libx11 libxkbcommon libxkbcommon-x11 \\ libxi libsm libice fontconfig freetype2 harfbuzz dbus libxrender \\ libxext libxrandr libxfixes libxcomposite libxdamage libxtst libxss \\ qt6-base qt6-multimedia Note On Arch, venv is part of the python package. No separate install needed. Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS System packages installed via apt (after sudo apt update ): sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip libpulse0 libasound2 \\ libegl1 libgl1 libopengl0 libglx-mesa0 libgl1-mesa-dri libegl1-mesa \\ libgles2 libxcb1 libxcb-cursor0 libxcb-icccm4 libxcb-image0 \\ libxcb-keysyms1 libxcb-randr0 libxcb-render0 libxcb-render-util0 \\ libxcb-shape0 libxcb-shm0 libxcb-sync1 libxcb-xfixes0 libxcb-xinerama0 \\ libxcb-xkb1 libxcb-util1 libx11-6 libx11-xcb1 libxkbcommon0 \\ libxkbcommon-x11-0 libxi6 libsm6 libice6 libfontconfig1 libfreetype6 \\ libharfbuzz0b libdbus-1-3 libxrender1 libxext6 libxrandr2 libxfixes3 \\ libxcomposite1 libxdamage1 libxtst6 libxss1 The list is installed in one apt install ; a package n"}, {"url": "hardware-managers.html", "title": "Hardware Managers", "subtitle": "ESP32 Volume Knob, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor, and the VolumeController they all feed", "headings": [], "body": "Hardware Managers - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Managers Hardware Managers ESP32 Volume Knob, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor, and the VolumeController they all feed Overview & Platform Availability Three managers talk to physical peripherals, and a fourth object routes volume to every output. Each is a PySide6 QObject in backend/ , created in main.py , given the settings manager through connect_settings_manager() , and registered as a QML context property. Class QML name File Logger Transport ESP32VolumeManager esp32VolumeManager backend/esp32_volume_manager.py octave.esp32_volume_manager USB serial (pyserial) BerryIMUManager berryIMU backend/berryimu_manager.py octave.berryimu_manager I2C bus 2 ( /dev/i2c-2 ) via smbus2 GestureManager gestureSensor backend/gesture_manager.py octave.gesture_manager I2C bus 2 ( /dev/i2c-2 ) via smbus2 (or legacy smbus ) VolumeController volumeController backend/volume_utils.py \u2014 \u2014 (routes 0\u2013100 % to every audio output) Logger names come from get_logger(__name__) in backend/logging_config.py , which turns backend.foo into octave.foo . Each manager's cleanup() runs from cleanup_on_quit() in main.py , connected to aboutToQuit ; SIGTERM and SIGINT call app.quit() , so they reach it too. What runs where Platform ESP32 knob BerryIMU Gesture sensor Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi Yes Yes Yes Windows / macOS Yes Inert. smbus2 is only installed on Linux ( requirements.txt : smbus2>=0.4.3; sys_platform == \"linux\" ), so the import in _start() fails, the manager emits \"Disconnected\" and never retries. (The gesture manager also tries the legacy smbus module before giving up.) Threads Manager Background thread What stays on the GUI thread ESP32VolumeManager A daemon threading.Thread running _read_loop , blocking in readline() ; started on every successful connect Opening the port, all five timers, every write (volume, mute, colour, keepalive) BerryIMUManager A daemon threading.Thread running _read_loop (gyro calibration, then the paced loop) Opening the bus and initialising the three chips in _start() , retries (scheduled with run_on_main ) GestureManager A daemon threading.Thread running _read_loop Opening the bus, wake-up, chip-ID check and the 219-entry init table in _start() , retries Signals emitted on a read thread (knob ticks, IMU values, gestures, the \"Error\" status after a failed read) reach QML and the main.py handlers as queued connections, because the managers live on the GUI thread. State changes that must happen on the GUI thread are handed back with run_on_main() ( backend/qt_threading.py ). ESP32 Volume Manager ESP32VolumeManager provides wireless rotary-encoder volume control. Per the module docstring two ESP32 boards are involved: an encoder unit with the knob, and an ESP32-S3 \"receiver dongle\" that plugs into the computer over USB and forwards the encoder's commands as newline-terminated text. OCTAVE only ever talks to the dongle. Linux Windows macOS Firmware is not in this repository The ESP32 sketches are not part of the OCTAVE tree (there are no .ino / PlatformIO sources here). What this page documents is the contract the manager implements: the serial parameters, the incoming command grammar and the outgoing LED commands. The wireless link and LED behaviour are up to the firmware. ESP-NOW Topology +-------------------+ ESP-NOW +-------------------+ | ESP32 Encoder | ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~> | ESP32-S3 Dongle | | (Rotary Knob) | (Wireless) | (USB Receiver) | | - Rotary encoder | | - USB serial | | - Button press | | - LED ring | +-------------------+ +--------+----------+ | USB Serial 115200 | +--------v----------+ | OCTAVE | | ESP32VolumeManager| +-------------------+ ESP-NOW is Espressif's connectionless peer-to-peer link; the encoder unit sends + / - / M style commands to the dongle, which forwards them over USB. The link between the two ESP32s is invisible to OCTAVE. Serial Communication The port is opened with serial.Serial(port, baudrate=115200, timeout=1, write_timeout=1) ; everything else is pyserial's default (8 data bits, no parity, one stop bit, no flow control). A daemon thread runs _read_loop : readline() blocks for up to the 1 s port timeout (the GIL is released while it waits); a timeout returns b\"\" and the loop re-checks _stop_thread . Bytes are decoded as UTF-8 (errors ignored) into a buffer and split on \\n ; a readline() that times out mid-line returns the partial bytes, which stay in the buffer until the newline arrives. Each non-empty line is stripped, upper-cased and handed to _process_command() . If the buffer still holds more than ESP32_MAX_LINE_BYTES (256) with no newline, it is line noise: it is dropped with one warning per connection, and the reader resyncs by discarding everything up to the next \\n . Covered by tests/test_esp32_read_buffer.py . A serial.SerialException logs serial read error , queues _handle_disconnect on the GUI thread with run_on_main() and ends the thread. Any other exception is logged and the"}, {"url": "hardware-setup.html", "title": "Hardware Setup", "subtitle": "Wiring, serial protocol, I2C bus, permissions, mounts and first-run verification for the ESP32 volume knob, BerryIMU v3 and PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor.", "headings": [{"id": "platforms", "text": "What Works Where", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32-volume", "text": "ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob", "level": 2}, {"id": "i2c-bus", "text": "I2C Bus 2 &amp; Permissions", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryimu", "text": "BerryIMU v3 IMU Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture-sensor", "text": "PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "verify", "text": "Verifying the Install (Log Lines)", "level": 2}, {"id": "3d-mounts", "text": "3D Printed Mounts", "level": 2}, {"id": "reference-install", "text": "Reference Install: Orange Pi 5 Plus", "level": 2}, {"id": "power", "text": "Power &amp; Vehicle Installation", "level": 2}, {"id": "why", "text": "Why It Is Set Up This Way", "level": 2}], "body": "Hardware Setup - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Setup Hardware Setup Wiring, serial protocol, I2C bus, permissions, mounts and first-run verification for the ESP32 volume knob, BerryIMU v3 and PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor. What Works Where This page is the physical-side companion to Hardware Managers , which documents the driver code in backend/ . The app runs on Linux (including Raspberry Pi and other single-board computers), Windows and macOS. Peripheral Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi Windows / macOS ESP32 volume knob (serial) Yes Yes BerryIMU v3 (I2C) Yes No \u2014 reports \"Disconnected\" and never retries PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor (I2C) Yes No \u2014 reports \"Disconnected\" and never retries The I2C drivers use Linux /dev/i2c-* devices through smbus2 , which requirements.txt installs only on Linux. The timing numbers quoted in the IMU driver's comments were measured on an Orange Pi 5 Plus . ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob Linux Windows macOS The manager's docstring describes two ESP32 boards: a remote encoder unit (rotary encoder + push button) and an ESP32-S3 receiver dongle plugged into the computer over USB. The encoder talks to the dongle over ESP-NOW; the dongle re-emits the commands as text lines on its serial port and drives an LED ring from the replies. OCTAVE only sees the serial port, so any device that speaks the protocol below on a serial port works. Architecture [Rotary Encoder] ESP-NOW (2.4 GHz) [USB Serial] [ + Button ] ----> [ESP32 Dev Board] ~~~~> [ESP32-S3 Dongle] ----> Computer \"Encoder Unit\" \"Receiver\" (OCTAVE) Sends: +, -, M + LED ring 115200 baud Forwards to USB Firmware is not in this repository There are no ESP32 sketches under the OCTAVE tree. The table below is the protocol backend/esp32_volume_manager.py implements; the wireless link and LED behaviour depend on compatible firmware. Serial Protocol 115200 baud with pyserial's defaults (8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, no flow control) and a 1 s read / write timeout. Every command is one ASCII line terminated by \\n ; OCTAVE trims and upper-cases incoming lines before parsing, and drops a partial line that grows past 256 bytes without a newline. Line Direction Meaning + or +N Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Volume up by 1 or N encoder ticks - or -N Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Volume down by 1 or N ticks M Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Mute toggle (button press) P Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Reserved for play/pause (double-click); accepted and logged, no action V<0-100> OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle Current volume percent for the LED ring (debounced to at most 20/s; also used as the 2 s keepalive) M0 / M1 OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle Mute indicator off / on C<r>,<g>,<b> OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle LED colour, e.g. C255,128,0 Each tick is multiplied by the esp32VolumeStepSize setting (default 1 %, adjustable 0.25\u201310 % in the Volume Knob settings); fractional results accumulate until a whole percent is available, then volumeController.applyVolume() moves every audio output together. Detection and port selection The dongle is recognised by Espressif's USB vendor ID 0x303A (on Windows also by \"JTAG\" / \"ESP32-S3\" in the port description, on macOS by \"JTAG\"). In Settings \u2192 Accessories \u2192 Volume Knob it is listed first and labelled \" <port> - ESP32-S3 (Recommended) \". The OBD port is never offered. Linux: the VID match via pyserial, plus every /dev/ttyUSB* and /dev/ttyACM* device. Hardware UARTs ( /dev/ttyS* ) are not scanned; to use one, set the port in the settings. macOS: VID match plus /dev/tty.usbserial* , /dev/cu.usbserial* , /dev/tty.usbmodem* , /dev/cu.usbmodem* , /dev/tty.SLAB* , /dev/cu.SLAB* , /dev/tty.wchusbserial* , /dev/cu.wchusbserial* . Windows: every COM port; the ESP32-S3 by VID or description. First run: the port is picked for you The esp32VolumePort setting defaults to \"\" (empty), and an empty setting means \"auto-select\": on start OCTAVE scans, saves the detected ESP32-S3 port (or, failing that, the first scanned port that is not the OBD port) and connects. Only override it in the Volume Knob settings if the scan picked the wrong device; OCTAVE saves the choice and reconnects 500 ms later. From then on it connects 3 s after every launch. Connection behaviour Startup: a 3 s delay, a port scan, then connect_device() if esp32VolumeEnabled is true. Unplug: detected when a read raises serial.SerialException , or when the 2 s keepalive write fails. With esp32AutoReconnect on, up to 5 attempts at 3, 6, 9, 10, 10 s ( min(10 s, 3 s \u00d7 n) ); the Accessories page shows \"Reconnecting in Ns... (n/5)\" and finally \"Max retries reached. Check connection.\" Pressing Connect there resets the counter. While disconnected, a background scan every 10 s keeps the port list fresh. Changing the port disconnects and reconnects 500 ms later. The enable toggle starts or stops the feature. LED ring modes Theme ( esp32LedColorMode = \"theme\" , default): the accent colour extracted from the current album art is sent as C whenever the album-art colours change. Static ( \"static\" ): a fixed colour from the picker ( esp32LedStaticColor , default #00FFFF ). LED"}, {"url": "index.html", "title": "OCTAVE Wiki", "subtitle": "Guides and reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience,\n            as implemented by its Python / PySide6 backend and the QML frontend it drives.", "headings": [], "body": "OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki Guides and reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience, as implemented by its Python / PySide6 backend and the QML frontend it drives. Frozen snapshot This wiki describes the python-final branch: the last state (2026-10-18, version 0.9.5) of OCTAVE's Python backend. It is a frozen snapshot and is not maintained; current OCTAVE development happens in C++ on the main branch. What is OCTAVE? OCTAVE is an open-source (GPLv3) car head unit / infotainment system you run on your own hardware. On this branch it is a Python / PySide6 backend ( backend/ and main.py ) driving a QML frontend ( frontend/ ). It plays local music, reads live data from the car over OBD-II, mirrors an Android phone over USB or Wi-Fi, shows a live 3D model of the vehicle from an IMU, and recolours its interface to match the album art. The Python backend runs from a source checkout on Linux, Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi class boards, Windows and macOS; it is not packaged and does not run on Android or iOS. The typical installation is a single-board computer behind the dash, a touchscreen, a power source and a 3D-printed mount. Development and testing can happen on any desktop OS. This reference describes the checked-out source. Optional hardware, optional Python packages and the Qt modules in your PySide6 install affect which features are available; the detailed pages call out the platform limits. Core Features \u266b Local Media Player MP3, M4A, MP4, FLAC, OGG, Opus and WAV playback with album art carousel, shuffle/repeat, playlists, a persistent metadata store, and album-art colour extraction. >_ OBD-II Diagnostics Live vehicle data from an ELM327 adapter over USB or Bluetooth serial through python-obd, DTC read and clear, freeze frame, a full-screen shift light, and a protocol transcript in the debug log. \u25d4 Gauges & Dashboards Seven gauge primitives plus G-force and compass widgets, JSON-defined dashboards (five built-in presets plus your own), a chooser with live miniatures, and an in-app drag-and-drop dashboard editor. \u260f Phone Mirror & Android Auto Mirror an Android phone over USB or Wi-Fi with the built-in scrcpy-protocol client (PyAV decode). Android Auto uses Google's Desktop Head Unit, embedded into OCTAVE's window on Windows only. \u21a7 Music Download Search YouTube Music and download tracks through the vendored music_dl package (yt-dlp, ytmusicapi). Finished downloads trigger a library rescan. \u2699 Hardware Integrations ESP32 wireless volume knob (ESP-NOW pair, USB dongle, LED ring), BerryIMU v3 attitude and heading readings, and PAJ7620U2 gesture recognition (I\u00b2C sensors on Linux). \u26df Live 3D Vehicle A rigged Jeep TJ in Qt Quick 3D with working doors, hood, tailgate, lights, steering and wheels, rolled and pitched in real time by the IMU. \u25c6 Dynamic Theming UI colours adapt in real time to album art. Accent, background and text colours are extracted and applied across every view (and to the ESP32 LED ring). \u223f Waveform Visualizer FFT visualisation with 96 frequency bars for local tracks or live mirrored phone audio, rendered in QML. \u270e Diagnostics & Logs Rotating log files, crash tracebacks and faulthandler dumps, a UI-thread watchdog, and a Diagnostics card that copies or exports the logs straight from the dash. \u21bb Network & Updates WiFi status, GitHub update checks and git-based self-update with in-app restart for checkout installs. Platform Support Every platform runs the same way: from a checkout, in a venv, with python setup.py or python main.py (see Running from Source ). Platform Notes Linux (Debian/Ubuntu, Arch, Fedora families) setup.py installs the Qt runtime libraries with the distro package manager (sudo). I\u00b2C sensors need an accessible bus and smbus2 (installed on Linux only). The Bluetooth OBD adapter finder (BlueZ over D-Bus) is Linux-only. Android Auto cannot be embedded (window lookup is Win32-only). Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi (Linux aarch64) The in-vehicle target; setup.py recognises a Raspberry Pi from the device-tree model and treats it as Linux. Google publishes no aarch64 adb , so phone mirroring needs an adb installed separately. Windows 10+ No system packages are installed by setup.py . I\u00b2C hardware (BerryIMU, gesture sensor) is unavailable. Android Auto's DHU window is embedded with Win32 calls and works only here. macOS (Intel and Apple Silicon) No system packages are installed by setup.py . I\u00b2C hardware and Android Auto embedding are unavailable. Wiki Sections Getting Started Installation & First Run \u2014 Running from a checkout, requirements, directory layout Architecture Overview \u2014 Manager pattern, startup in main.py , threading, volume, video paths, logging, design decisions Backend Deep Dives Settings Manager \u2014 Central settings store, property system, coalesced JSON persistence Media Manager \u2014 Local audio playback, library scanning, album art and colours OBD Manager \u2014 python-obd connection, supported parameters, threaded worker Network Manager \u2014 WiFi status,"}, {"url": "media-manager.html", "title": "Media Manager", "subtitle": "Local media playback in backend/media_manager.py: QMediaPlayer, folder-based playlists, mutagen tags and a persistent tag store, cover-art caching, album-art colour theming, volume and ducking, playback resume and recovery", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "module", "text": "Module and Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-formats", "text": "Supported Audio Formats", "level": 2}, {"id": "qmediaplayer", "text": "QMediaPlayer Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "library-scanning", "text": "Library Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "metadata-caching", "text": "Metadata Caching and the Tag Store", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art", "text": "Album Art Extraction", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-extraction", "text": "Color Extraction and Theming", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-control", "text": "Playback Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle-repeat", "text": "Shuffle Logic", "level": 2}, {"id": "playlist-management", "text": "Playlist Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "display-names", "text": "Display Names", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume-mute", "text": "Volume, Mute and Ducking", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback State Resume", "level": 2}, {"id": "precaching", "text": "Neighbor Pre-caching", "level": 2}, {"id": "error-recovery", "text": "Error Recovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "extending", "text": "How to Modify / Extend", "level": 2}], "body": "Media Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Media Manager Media Manager Local media playback in backend/media_manager.py: QMediaPlayer, folder-based playlists, mutagen tags and a persistent tag store, cover-art caching, album-art colour theming, volume and ducking, playback resume and recovery Overview The MediaManager class in backend/media_manager.py handles all local audio playback. It wraps Qt's QMediaPlayer and QAudioOutput , turns the sub-folders of the music folder into playlists, keeps a metadata and album-art cache backed by a persistent tag store on disk, extracts dominant colors from album art for the \"Album Art Capture\" theme, and provides shuffle, skip, playback resume and automatic recovery from unplayable files. main.py creates one instance, calls media_manager.connect_settings_manager(settings_manager) and registers it as the QML context property mediaManager . Playback controls are Slots and state changes are Signals; the QML that uses them is frontend/MediaRoom.qml , MediaPlayer.qml , BottomBar.qml , MainMenu.qml , DownloadPage.qml , settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml and Main.qml . Desktop Module and Dependencies Piece What it is Playback PySide6.QtMultimedia : QMediaPlayer , QAudioOutput , QMediaDevices Tags and covers mutagen 1.47.0 (pinned in requirements.txt ): mutagen.mp3.MP3 / mutagen.id3.ID3 , mutagen.mp4.MP4 , mutagen.flac.FLAC / Picture , mutagen.oggvorbis.OggVorbis , mutagen.oggopus.OggOpus , mutagen.wave.WAVE , and the generic mutagen.File for duration only. Only MP3 and ID3 are imported at module load; the others are imported inside the functions that use them Colour extraction QImage plus the standard colorsys module; no Pillow and no numpy Disk writes atomic_write_text() / atomic_write_bytes() from backend/atomic_io.py (temp file in the same directory, fsync , os.replace , then fsync of the directory except on Windows) Storage paths get_app_data_dir() from backend/settings_manager.py ( %APPDATA%/OCTAVE , ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE , or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE falling back to ~/.config/OCTAVE ) Logging get_logger(__name__) from backend/logging_config.py Module-level helpers: AUDIO_EXTENSIONS , strip_audio_extension(filename) , is_safe_path(base, target) and sanitize_metadata(text, max_length=200) . Supported Audio Formats The library scanner only picks up files whose extension is in AUDIO_EXTENSIONS : # backend/media_manager.py AUDIO_EXTENSIONS = ( '.mp3' , '.m4a' , '.mp4' , '.flac' , '.ogg' , '.opus' , '.wav' ) .mp4 is listed because YouTube serves audio-only streams in an MP4 container that yt-dlp names with a literal .mp4 extension. strip_audio_extension(filename) removes any of these extensions (and only these) and is what the display-name and metadata fallbacks use. Tags are read by _read_tags(file_path, base_name) , which never raises, and covers by _extract_art_for(file_path, album_id) . Both dispatch on the lower-cased extension. Both also accept .aac in the MP4 branch, but .aac is not in AUDIO_EXTENSIONS , so the scanner never lists such a file. A file that fails to open yields the file-name title with \"Unknown Artist\" / \"Unknown Album\" and duration 0. Whether a file actually plays depends on the codecs available to Qt Multimedia in the installed PySide6. Format Tags ( _read_tags ) Cover ( _extract_art_for ) MP3 MP3(path) : TIT2 , TPE1 , TALB ; length from mp3.info First APIC frame with data ( _extract_album_art_mp3 ) M4A / MP4 (/ AAC) MP4(path) : \\xa9nam , \\xa9ART , \\xa9alb First covr item; PNG if imageformat == MP4Cover.FORMAT_PNG , else JPEG ( _extract_album_art_mp4 ) FLAC Vorbis comments title , artist , album via FLAC First PICTURE block ( FLAC.pictures , _extract_album_art_flac ) Ogg Vorbis Vorbis comments via OggVorbis First decodable base64 FLAC picture in the metadata_block_picture comment ( _extract_album_art_ogg ) Opus Vorbis comments via OggOpus ( _open_xiph_file picks the class by extension) As for Ogg Vorbis WAV The optional ID3 chunk via WAVE : TIT2 , TPE1 , TALB None anything else mutagen.File for duration only; title from the file name None QMediaPlayer Integration The constructor creates one QMediaPlayer and one QAudioOutput and sets the output to volume 0.5 (the saved volume is applied moments later by VolumeController in main.py , see Volume ). It also sets the dynamic properties probe-size (10 MB) and analyzeduration (5 s) on the player; the code does not check whether the active Qt multimedia backend reads them. Output device. A QAudioOutput stays bound to the device that was the system default when it was created. The constructor therefore creates a QMediaDevices and connects audioOutputsChanged to _follow_default_output() , which switches the output to QMediaDevices.defaultAudioOutput() whenever that differs from the current device (a null device is ignored) and logs \"Audio output changed, now using: ...\". The comment names the cases: HDMI audio across a display sleep, a USB DAC. # backend/media_manager.py \u2014 __init__ (abridged) self._playe"}, {"url": "network-manager.html", "title": "Network Manager", "subtitle": "Connectivity polling with offline backoff, ancestry-aware GitHub update checks, and git-based self-update of the source checkout", "headings": [], "body": "Network Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Network Manager Network Manager Connectivity polling with offline backoff, ancestry-aware GitHub update checks, and git-based self-update of the source checkout Overview The NetworkManager has three jobs: report whether the head unit is online (and on which network), tell the user when the main branch on GitHub has moved past the running commit, and -- because OCTAVE's Python backend runs from a git checkout -- pull that update in place and restart. It is defined in backend/network_manager.py and registered as the QML context property networkManager in main.py . Thread Safety The routine connectivity, update-check and update-pipeline work runs on a ThreadPoolExecutor with a single worker, and results are polled back on the main thread by a 250 ms QTimer so Qt signals are only ever emitted from the GUI thread. A few short calls still run synchronously on the GUI thread: the startup git --version probe, the click-time git status --porcelain check in applySelfUpdate() , and the py_compile check in restartApp() . The manager targets the GitHub repository RobDeGeorge/OCTAVE ( GITHUB_REPO ) and compares the local git HEAD against the tip of the remote main branch. Architecture NetworkManager (backend/network_manager.py) | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (1 worker) -- one job at a time (_pending_future) | |-- _do_network_check() nmcli/networksetup/netsh + generate_204 | |-- _do_update_check_sync() GitHub API + git ancestry queries | |-- _do_self_update_sync() fetch / reset / py_compile / pip / log | |-- QTimer _poll_timer 30 s online, 120 s after 3 misses |-- QTimer _result_poll_timer 250 ms: future done? progress message? |-- QTimer.singleShot(2000) _startup_probe: git --version + first check | |-- Signals --> QML (all emitted from the main thread) Background Work Pattern Only one background job runs at a time. _submit_work() takes a callable and a result-type tag, submits it to the executor, and starts the result poll timer: def _submit_work ( self , fn, result_type): \"\"\"Submit blocking work to the thread pool. Only one job at a time.\"\"\" if self ._pending_future and not self ._pending_future.done(): return # Already busy self ._pending_future = self ._executor.submit(fn) self ._pending_future._result_type = result_type self ._result_poll_timer.start() Each tick of _check_pending_result() first relays any pending progress message, then -- if the future is done -- stops the timer, unwraps the result and routes it by tag: \"network_status\" , \"update_check\" or \"self_update\" . An exception from the job maps to updateStatus = \"error\" (\"Could not reach GitHub\") or selfUpdateStatus = \"error\" (\"Update failed unexpectedly\"). A consequence of the single slot: any job submitted while another is still running is silently dropped. A network poll that lands during a self-update is simply skipped; a checkForUpdates() tap that lands while a connectivity probe is running sets updateStatus to \"checking\" but never submits the check, so the status stays at \"checking\" until the next tap. Connectivity Detection Network status is polled every 30 seconds while online. After three consecutive failed probes the interval backs off to 2 minutes and returns to 30 s on the first success ( POLL_ONLINE_MS / POLL_OFFLINE_MS / OFFLINE_STREAK_FOR_BACKOFF ). The first probe runs 2 s after startup so the UI can paint first; the git --version probe behind canSelfUpdate runs at that same moment rather than in the constructor. The name lookup and the internet probe run one after the other inside one worker job. Network Name Detection _get_network_name_sync() shells out to the platform's network tool. The WiFi command runs first; if it yields no SSID a second command looks for a wired link and reports the literal string \"Ethernet\" . Platform WiFi SSID Wired fallback Linux nmcli -t -f ACTIVE,SSID dev wifi list --rescan no -- first line starting yes: . Without --rescan no nmcli rescans when the last scan is over 30 s old, taking the radio off channel for ~4 s; on the Pi that stalled phone mirroring over the OCTAVE-Jeep hotspot, which shares the radio, once a minute. nmcli -t -f TYPE,STATE dev -- a line containing both ethernet and connected macOS networksetup -getairportnetwork en0 -- text after Current Wi-Fi Network: ifconfig en0 -- output contains status: active Windows netsh wlan show interfaces -- first SSID line that is not BSSID netsh interface show interface -- a line containing Connected and Ethernet Every name-lookup subprocess has a 5-second timeout. Any exception (missing tool, timeout) is logged at debug level and the name comes back empty; when neither WiFi nor Ethernet is found the name is also empty, and networkNameChanged(\"\") is emitted if a name was previously shown, so a stale SSID is never left on screen. Internet Connectivity Check Reachability is a single HTTPS GET of Google's generate_204 endpoint (a captive-portal probe URL that returns an empty 204) using urllib.request.urlopen with a 5 s timeout. "}, {"url": "obd-manager.html", "title": "OBD Manager", "subtitle": "OBD-II vehicle diagnostics on python-obd: a threaded connection worker, demand-driven watchers for 83 base commands and 37 extended values, DTC reading, Bluetooth adapter pairing, and the parameter model that feeds every gauge", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "architecture", "text": "Files and Threads", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-consumers", "text": "QML Consumers", "level": 2}, {"id": "connection-flow", "text": "Connection Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "threaded-worker", "text": "Threaded Connection Worker", "level": 2}, {"id": "elm-init", "text": "ELM327 Initialisation and Connection Status", "level": 2}, {"id": "speed-optimizations", "text": "python-obd Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "device-discovery", "text": "Device Discovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "unified-adapter-api", "text": "Unified Adapter API", "level": 2}, {"id": "direct-bluetooth", "text": "Direct Bluetooth (rfcomm://, Linux)", "level": 3}, {"id": "adapter-finder", "text": "Adapter Finder: Scan, Pair, Connect", "level": 2}, {"id": "auto-reconnect", "text": "Auto-Reconnect, Passive Scanning and the Connection Monitor", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-watchdog", "text": "Data Watchdog", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-parameters", "text": "Supported OBD Parameters", "level": 2}, {"id": "multi-value-pids", "text": "Extended PIDs 0x61 and up (37 values from 23 PIDs)", "level": 3}, {"id": "vin", "text": "Vehicle identity (VIN)", "level": 3}, {"id": "watcher-system", "text": "Which PIDs Get Polled", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-scanning", "text": "Vehicle Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnostics", "text": "Diagnostics (DTC)", "level": 2}, {"id": "elm327-protocol", "text": "ELM327 Protocol Module", "level": 2}, {"id": "protocol-logging", "text": "Protocol Logging", "level": 2}, {"id": "parameter-model", "text": "OBDParameterModel and IMU Parameters", "level": 2}, {"id": "shift-light", "text": "Shift Light, Full-Screen Flash and Aura", "level": 2}, {"id": "demo-mode", "text": "Demo and Simulated Data", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-keys", "text": "Settings Keys", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Signals and Properties", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "known-gaps", "text": "Known Gaps", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "OBD Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / OBD Manager OBD Manager OBD-II vehicle diagnostics on python-obd: a threaded connection worker, demand-driven watchers for 83 base commands and 37 extended values, DTC reading, Bluetooth adapter pairing, and the parameter model that feeds every gauge Overview OBDManager ( backend/obd_manager.py ) provides real-time vehicle data over the OBD-II port through an ELM327-compatible adapter. main.py creates it with the settings manager and registers it as the QML context property obdManager through install_obd_manager() , which dev mode uses to swap in a simulator instead ( below ). The adapter I/O is done by the python-obd library ( obd>=0.7.3 in requirements.txt ; the descriptions of library behaviour on this page were checked against 0.7.3). obd.Async owns the serial port, initialises the ELM327, negotiates the protocol, polls the watched commands on its own thread and calls back into the manager for every reply. The manager adds what python-obd does not do: a non-blocking connect, port discovery, reconnect policy, demand-driven watch lists, multi-value PIDs above 0x5F, VIN decoding, a diagnostic mode and the QML-facing signals. The QML pages ( OBDMenu , OBDHome , OBDDiagnostics , HomeOBDView , the OBD settings page, the adapter sheet and the gauge/dashboard system ) talk only to obdManager and obdAdapterFinder . Desktop: Linux, Windows, macOS, Raspberry Pi Files and Threads File Description backend/obd_manager.py OBDManager , OBDConnectionWorker , compute_poll_params() , extended_command() , probe_vehicle() and the VIN command. 83 obd.commands.* entries in _get_all_commands() . The obd module is imported lazily ( lazy_module(\"obd\") from backend/lazy_import.py ) because python-obd pulls in pint, which the source comment puts at about 0.5 s on the Pi. backend/elm327_protocol.py Pure-Python ELM327 protocol code (no I/O). At runtime the manager imports only EXTENDED_PID_TABLE , decode_extended_pid() and decode_vin() ; the rest (base PID table, init table, response/DTC parsers, ResponseBuffer ) is reference code exercised by the tests. See ELM327 Protocol Module . backend/obd_transports/ The rfcomm:// URL helpers and a pyserial URL handler ( protocol_rfcomm.py ) for a direct Bluetooth RFCOMM socket. See Direct Bluetooth . backend/obd_adapter_finder.py OBDAdapterFinder (QML obdAdapterFinder ) plus _BluezPinAgent and _BluezPairer : Bluetooth discovery with PySide6 QtBluetooth , PIN pairing and trust through BlueZ over QtDBus , then hand-off to connect_direct() . Linux only. backend/qt_threading.py run_on_main(fn, delay_ms=0) : queues a callable onto the GUI thread from any thread. Used by every OBD background thread, because QTimer.singleShot called from a plain threading.Thread never fires. frontend/OBDAdapterSheet.qml The \"Connect OBD2 Adapter\" sheet the finder drives. frontend/OBDParameterModel.qml Singleton that maps every OBD signal to a paramId and holds the live paramValues map every gauge binds to (see below ). dev/mock_obd_manager.py MockOBDManager , the dev-mode simulator. The dev/ directory is gitignored: it exists in the author's working tree, not in a fresh clone. Threads Thread Created by What runs there GUI thread Qt The manager, its four QTimer s, every slot QML calls, _on_connection_complete() , _refresh_watchers() , and enter_diagnostic_mode() (which blocks it, see Diagnostics ). Connect thread _start_connection() : a fresh QThread per attempt OBDConnectionWorker.do_connect() : the blocking obd.Async(...) constructor and, on success, probe_vehicle() . Results come back as queued signals. python-obd Async thread connection.start() python-obd's polling loop: queries every watched command in turn and runs the callbacks, so the parameter signals ( rpmChanged , obdParameterChanged , ...) are emitted from this thread and reach QML as queued connections. Connection monitor _start_connection_monitor() , daemon threading.Thread _monitor_connection() : every 2 s checks connection.status() and the port file ( below ). Short-lived daemon threads scan_vehicle() , read_dtc() , read_current_dtc() , read_freeze_frame() , clear_dtc() , read_status() , read_vin() One thread per call; results reach the GUI thread through private signals or run_on_main() . obd-rfcomm-bind _start_rfcomm_bind() The rfcomm bind subprocess on Linux ( below ). +---------------------------+ queued signals +----------------------------+ | OBDManager (GUI thread) | <----------------------- | OBDConnectionWorker | | timers, slots, QML API | | (QThread, one per attempt) | +-------------+-------------+ | obd.Async(port, ...) | | | probe_vehicle() | | watch() / start() / stop() +----------------------------+ v +---------------------------+ callbacks emit the parameter signals | obd.Async polling thread | ------------------------------------------> QML | (python-obd) | +-------------+-------------+ | pyserial (serial_for_url) /dev/rfcomm*, /dev/ttyUSB*, /dev/ttyACM*, COM*, /dev/tty.*, rfcomm://MAC | +-------"}, {"url": "pages.html", "title": "Pages", "subtitle": "Every view that can be pushed on the StackView (plus the window-level Now Playing Studio): what it shows, how it is reached, which managers it talks to, and the QML techniques worth knowing", "headings": [{"id": "inventory", "text": "View Inventory", "level": 2}, {"id": "mainmenu", "text": "MainMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaroom", "text": "MediaRoom.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaplayer", "text": "MediaPlayer.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloadpage", "text": "DownloadPage.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdhome", "text": "OBDHome.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdmenu", "text": "OBDMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obddiagnostics", "text": "OBDDiagnostics.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensorhome", "text": "SensorHome.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensormenu", "text": "SensorMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "carmenu", "text": "CarMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "clockmenu", "text": "ClockMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "phonemirror", "text": "PhoneMirrorView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "androidauto", "text": "AndroidAutoView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "settingsmenu", "text": "SettingsMenu and its Layouts", "level": 2}, {"id": "nowplayingstudio", "text": "NowPlayingStudio.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation-summary", "text": "Navigation Summary", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Pages - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Pages Pages Every view that can be pushed on the StackView (plus the window-level Now Playing Studio): what it shows, how it is reached, which managers it talks to, and the QML techniques worth knowing View Inventory Most views live at the top of frontend/ and are built once, on first visit or during prewarming, then retained by the page cache. DownloadPage uses a separate cache in the media views; the dashboard editor lives under dashboards/editor/ and is destroyed when popped (see the page cache ). The table lists what each one declares and where it is opened from; \"required\" means required property , which cachedPage() satisfies by always passing stackView and mainWindow . Because instances are long-lived, per-visit work belongs in StackView.onActivated , not Component.onCompleted . View objectName Properties it takes Reached from MainMenu mainMenu stackView , mainWindow , windowWidth , windowHeight (plain) StackView.initialItem ; BottomBar Home normally pops back to it; an open wiki reader can require a cached Home above Settings MediaRoom mediaRoom stackView , mainWindow (plain) BottomBar Media, MainMenu media panel, MediaPlayer row tap, DownloadPage play MediaPlayer mediaPlayer stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar Media (toggles with MediaRoom), MainMenu media panel DownloadPage downloadPage stackView (plain), mainWindow (plain) openDownloadPage() in MediaRoom and MediaPlayer (cached instance) OBDHome obdHome stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar OBD OBDMenu obdMenu stackView , mainWindow (required) OBDHome \"Dashboards\" card, MainMenu OBD panel, BottomBar OBD via lastOBDPage OBDDiagnostics obdDiagnostics stackView , mainWindow (required) OBDHome \"Trouble Codes\" card, BottomBar OBD via lastOBDPage SensorHome sensorHome stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar Sensor button SensorMenu sensorMenu stackView , mainWindow (required) SensorHome \"Sensors\" card, BottomBar Sensor via lastSensorPage CarMenu carMenu stackView (required), mainWindow (plain) SensorHome \"3D View\" card, BottomBar Sensor via lastSensorPage ClockMenu \u2014 stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar clock; navigateTo(\"ClockMenu.qml\") (command server / screenshot sweeper) PhoneMirrorView phoneMirrorView stackView , mainWindow (plain) BottomBar Phone Mirror (when phoneMirrorEnabled and phoneMirrorManager.nativeAvailable ) AndroidAutoView \u2014 stackView , mainWindow (plain) navigateTo(\"AndroidAutoView.qml\") only; the BottomBar Android Auto button is hidden ( visible: false ) SettingsMenu settingsMenu stackView , mainWindow , initialSection (required) BottomBar Settings, navigateToSettings() DashboardEditor ( dashboards/editor/ ) \u2014 stackView , callerPage , optional editingId / draftJson OBDMenu chooser (+ New, Edit, \u25b2 Draft); see Gauges & Dashboards Every view uses the OBD or media colour tokens from Style and the scaled sizes from Spacing , and every one carries the local dp() / dpMin() shim described in GUI Scale . MainMenu.qml frontend/MainMenu.qml is the home screen and the initialItem of the StackView: \"Media on left (1/3), OBD on right (2/3)\". It imports QtQuick.Controls.Basic , Qt5Compat.GraphicalEffects and QtQuick.Effects \u2014 the only file besides MediaRoom that uses both effect modules. Layout RowLayout { anchors.fill : parent; anchors.margins : dp ( 10 ); spacing : dp ( 10 ) // LEFT: media (Layout.preferredWidth: parent.width * 0.33, clip: true) Rectangle { id : mediaSection ... } // RIGHT: OBD (Layout.fillWidth: true, 2 px accent border) Rectangle { HomeOBDView { anchors.fill : parent; anchors.margins : dp ( 5 ) } } } Media panel Blurred album art background \u2014 an Image of the current art with layer.effect: MultiEffect { blurEnabled: true; blurMax: 64; blur: min(1, settingsManager.backgroundBlurRadius / 64) } under a #C0000000 overlay; the panel border is drawn by a separate border-only rectangle at z: 10 so it stays crisp above the blur. Foreground album art with the file's single DropShadow (offset 4/4, radius 12, 25 samples). Scrolling title and \"artist \u2022 album\" \u2014 two copies of the Flickable + 3 s Timer + 5 s InOutQuad NumberAnimation ping-pong on contentX , running only while the text is wider than its container. Seek slider \u2014 a custom Slider (accent fill, round handle, userSeeking flag) that on release calls phoneMirrorManager.mediaSeek() while the phone has the controls and mediaManager.set_position() otherwise, flanked by position/duration labels. Dual source \u2014 usePhone is phoneMirrorManager.mediaActive (see phone media ); currentArt/Title/Artist/Album pick the phone's media session or private _local* copies (\"to avoid binding loops\"). Missing art falls back to assets/missing_art.png . Tap to open the player \u2014 a TapHandler , not a MouseArea , pushes MediaPlayer.qml or MediaRoom.qml according to settingsManager.musicButtonDefaultPage . The comment explains: the layout is full of a Flickable title, a Flickable metadata row and a Slider, \"all of which consume MouseArea clicks. TapHand"}, {"url": "phone-mirror-android-auto.html", "title": "Phone Mirror &amp; Android Auto", "subtitle": "The Python scrcpy-protocol client (PyAV decode, QVideoSink frames) with OCTAVE's forked phone server, and the Google DHU-based Android Auto path", "headings": [], "body": "Phone Mirror & Android Auto - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Phone Mirror & Android Auto Phone Mirror & Android Auto The Python scrcpy-protocol client (PyAV decode, QVideoSink frames) with OCTAVE's forked phone server, and the Google DHU-based Android Auto path Overview OCTAVE has two ways of putting a connected Android phone on the dash: Phone Mirror -- OCTAVE is its own scrcpy-protocol client ( backend/phone_mirror/ ). It pushes OCTAVE's forked scrcpy server to the phone over adb , decodes the H.264 stream with PyAV, plays the phone's audio, and injects real multitouch over the control socket. The server jar is in the checkout; adb and the av package must also be available (see nativeAvailable below). Android Auto -- Runs Google's Desktop Head Unit (DHU) as a separate process and embeds its window. In practice this works only on Windows; see What actually works . The two paths share one thing: adb , found once by the module-level _find_adb() in backend/phone_mirror/manager.py and reused by Android Auto. Phone Mirror pushes decoded frames into a QVideoSink bound to a QML VideoOutput ; Android Auto embeds a native window (Windows) and additionally registers a QQuickImageProvider ( image://dhuframe/ ) that no QML currently reads. Desktop only +-------------------------------+ USB/Wi-Fi adb +--------------------------------------+ | Android phone | <-----------> | OCTAVE (Python backend) | | | | | | octave-phone-server (jar) | video socket | ScrcpyClient (scrcpy_client.py) | | org.octave.phoneserver | -------------> | demux -> PyAV H.264 decode | | virtual display = view size | audio socket | -> QVideoFrame -> QVideoSink | | MediaCodec H.264 encoder | -------------> | PCM -> gain -> QAudioSink | | MirrorKeeper (sleep/panel) | control socket | touch / keys / keeper policy | | PhoneMedia (media session) | <------------> | PHONE_STATE / MEDIA_* messages | | | | | | | Google DHU (Android Auto) | tcp:5277 | PhoneMirrorManager -> PhoneMirrorView| | head unit server on phone | <-----------> | AndroidAutoManager -> AndroidAutoView| +-------------------------------+ +--------------------------------------+ Phone Mirror Phone Mirror shows an Android phone inside OCTAVE and lets the user drive it from the dash with real multitouch. OCTAVE is a scrcpy-protocol client : it pushes the phone server jar ( tools/phone-server/octave-phone-server ) to /data/local/tmp/octave-phone-server.jar with adb , starts it with app_process , decodes the H.264 stream and injects input over the control socket. The server is OCTAVE's fork of the scrcpy server ( phone_server/ , Apache-2.0, forked at v3.3.4, Java package org.octave.phoneserver , version string 3.3.4-octave ). It needs shell privileges, which only an adb-launched process gets, so it cannot be an installed app. There is no external scrcpy binary, v4l2loopback or ffmpeg subprocess in the mirroring path. Touch uses the control socket; wakePhone() and pressHome() on a virtual display still run one-off adb shell commands. File Class Role backend/phone_mirror/manager.py PhoneMirrorManager adb discovery, device probes, settings, session lifecycle, keeper policy, ducking, phone media, Wi-Fi / QR pairing; QML context property phoneMirrorManager backend/phone_mirror/scrcpy_client.py ScrcpyClient One session: server push/launch or attach, sockets, demux, PyAV decode, black-frame hold, audio playback, control and device messages backend/phone_mirror/adaptive_bitrate.py AdaptiveBitRate Pure logic for adaptive video bit rate, see Adaptive quality backend/phone_mirror/qrcodegen.py QrCode Project Nayuki's QR Code generator (MIT, credited in NOTICE ) for Wi-Fi pairing phone_server/src/main/java/org/octave/phoneserver/ Server , DesktopConnection , Streamer , Controller , MirrorKeeper , PhoneMedia , NewDisplayCapture , CleanUp The program running on the phone frontend/PhoneMirrorView.qml -- VideoOutput + MultiPointTouchArea + nav row, reconnect and asleep overlays, notice banner, phone picker, QR / error / retry screen frontend/PhoneQrPairingPanel.qml -- The QR code and pairing steps, used by the view and the settings modal frontend/settings/AccessoriesSettingsPage.qml -- Phone mirror settings card (Wi-Fi QR pairing, display mode and size, phone screen, audio, gain, ducking, status line) Logging: loggers octave.phone_mirror (manager) and octave.phone_mirror.scrcpy_client (client). Server log lines are relayed at INFO (lines containing WARN or ERROR at warning level) with a scrcpy server: prefix. Measured with the Python client on the Orange Pi 5 Plus ( docs/PHONE_MIRROR_NATIVE_PLAN.md , \"Phase 1 hardware results\"): handshake at +0.34 s, first decoded frame at +0.72\u20130.76 s and the picture at +1.13 s from the click, the same with the phone idle for 70 s; a touch reacts in 0.45 s; 23\u201328 % of one core while scrolling. Software H.264 decode at 1280x800 costs 12\u201315 % of one RK3588 core (PyAV's bundled ffmpeg). PhoneMirrorManager Owns the single session (one phone at a time), the adb probes the UI needs, and "}, {"url": "settings-manager.html", "title": "Settings Manager", "subtitle": "Central settings store of the Python backend: one JSON file, an in-memory cache served to QML through PySide6 properties, a coalesced atomic disk write, and a metadata registry that drives the Quick Panel", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "file-location", "text": "File Location and Storage Paths", "level": 2}, {"id": "property-signal-system", "text": "Property / Signal / Slot System", "level": 2}, {"id": "lifecycle", "text": "Startup, In-Memory Cache and Coalesced Writes", "level": 2}, {"id": "json-persistence", "text": "JSON Persistence and Validation", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-registry", "text": "Settings Registry Metadata System", "level": 2}, {"id": "default-settings", "text": "Default Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "key-methods", "text": "Key Methods and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "section-visibility", "text": "Section Visibility System", "level": 2}, {"id": "pinned-settings", "text": "Pinned Quick Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "generic-access", "text": "Generic Setting Access", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-colors", "text": "Album Art Colors", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-connection", "text": "Connection to QML and Other Managers", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-new-setting", "text": "How to Add a New Setting", "level": 2}, {"id": "signal-reference", "text": "Signal Reference", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Manager Settings Manager Central settings store of the Python backend: one JSON file, an in-memory cache served to QML through PySide6 properties, a coalesced atomic disk write, and a metadata registry that drives the Quick Panel Overview The SettingsManager class ( backend/settings_manager.py ) is the central configuration store for all of OCTAVE. It inherits from QObject and exposes typed settings to QML through @Property getters, change signals, and @Slot methods: 96 default keys, 79 properties, 83 signals and 143 slots. Everything is persisted in one file, settingsConfigure.json in the OS-specific app data directory. QML reaches it as the context property settingsManager . Beyond the typed properties, the manager provides a settings registry (25 entries) that maps a setting ID to its label, category, control type and save slot. The Quick Panel and the pin system render settings from this metadata, so a slider or toggle can be pinned without any per-setting QML. Since the 2026-09 performance sweep the manager is memory-served : the file is read once in the constructor, every getter answers from the in-memory copy, and every setter updates that copy and arms a 500 ms single-shot timer that performs one atomic write for the whole burst. Details in Startup, In-Memory Cache and Coalesced Writes . Linux / Windows / macOS File Location and Storage Paths The settings file lives in the directory returned by the module-level function get_app_data_dir() , which creates the directory if it is missing and picks the path by platform: Platform Path Rule in code Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE\\settingsConfigure.json APPDATA env var; falls back to the home directory if unset macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json hard-coded under the home directory Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi ~/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE when the variable is set (every platform that is not win32 or darwin ) def get_app_data_dir (): app_name = \"OCTAVE\" if sys.platform == 'win32' : base = os.environ.get( 'APPDATA' , os.path.expanduser( '~' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) elif sys.platform == 'darwin' : data_dir = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), 'Library' , 'Application Support' , app_name) else : base = os.environ.get( 'XDG_CONFIG_HOME' , os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), '.config' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) os.makedirs(data_dir, exist_ok= True ) return data_dir The same directory is the root for everything else OCTAVE persists; other managers import the helper ( backend/logging_config.py computes the same path itself, because logging is set up before the other backend modules are imported): Path under the app data dir Owner settingsConfigure.json SettingsManager (this page) theme_art/theme_<safe name>.<ext> SettingsManager, album art copied or downloaded for a custom theme ( save_custom_theme ) logs/ backend/logging_config.py writes the rotating logs; backend/diagnostics_manager.py reads and exports them (see Development ) dashboards/ DashboardManager user dashboards ( Gauges & Dashboards ) metadata_cache.json , display_names.json MediaManager temp/ PhoneMirrorManager (phone album art and queue artwork) Retired keys are deleted at startup Files from older builds may carry spotifyClientId , spotifyClientSecret , mediaSource (the removed Spotify integration) and scrcpyAudioEnabled (the removed phone audio toggle). The constructor deletes them, saves, and logs \"Removed settings of retired features\"; it then deletes the old Spotify login token: .spotify_token_cache in the app data dir and in backend/ , and the OCTAVE_Spotify OS keychain entry (through keyring , when it is installed). Property / Signal / Slot System Every setting with a dedicated property follows the same three-part pattern: Signal declaration named <key>Changed (e.g. themeSettingChanged ), carrying the new value where the value is scalar and no argument for dict/list settings Read-only property with the camelCase JSON key as its name and the signal as notify Save slot named save_<snake_case> that updates the backing member, calls update_setting() , which emits the signal From backend/settings_manager.py : # 1. Signal declaration (class level) themeSettingChanged = Signal( str ) # 2. Property with notify signal @Property( str , notify=themeSettingChanged) def themeSetting (self): return self._theme_setting # 3. Slot for QML to call @Slot( str ) def save_theme_setting (self, theme): logger.debug(f \"Saving theme setting: {theme}\" ) self._theme_setting = theme self.update_setting( \"themeSetting\" , theme, self.themeSettingChanged) The slot names are snake_case ( save_theme_setting , not saveThemeSetting ), and the QML is written against those names. The camelCase exceptions are setCurrentVolume() (called by VolumeController ) and the shutdown flush flushPendingSave() , which also has a snake_case alias flush_pending_save() ; main.py calls the a"}, {"url": "settings-reference.html", "title": "Settings Reference", "subtitle": "Every key in settingsConfigure.json as defined by the Python backend: type, default, allowed values, the UI page that owns it, and what it affects.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "device", "text": "Device and Window", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display", "level": 2}, {"id": "media", "text": "Media and Now Playing", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback Resume", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-parameters", "text": "OBD Parameter PIDs", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32", "text": "ESP32 Volume Knob", "level": 2}, {"id": "imu", "text": "IMU Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture", "text": "Gesture Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone", "text": "Android Auto and Phone Mirror", "level": 2}, {"id": "download", "text": "Downloads", "level": 2}, {"id": "ui-state", "text": "Settings UI State", "level": 2}, {"id": "generic", "text": "QML-Only Keys (no backend default)", "level": 2}, {"id": "in-memory", "text": "In-Memory Only", "level": 2}, {"id": "persistence", "text": "Persistence and File Format", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Reference Settings Reference Every key in settingsConfigure.json as defined by the Python backend: type, default, allowed values, the UI page that owns it, and what it affects. Overview Settings are owned by SettingsManager ( backend/settings_manager.py , QML context property settingsManager ) and persisted to settingsConfigure.json in the directory returned by get_app_data_dir() (created if missing): Windows: %APPDATA%/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json , which is ~/.config/OCTAVE/ when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset _default_settings defines 96 top-level keys (95 in the dict literal plus directoryHistory , added right after it). Settings are read once at startup, validated against the defaults, served from memory, and written back atomically 500 ms after the last change. The mechanics are on the Settings Manager page; this page is the key catalogue. How to read the tables: Property : the QML-visible property name (same as the JSON key) or how QML reaches a key that has no property. Type : the Python type of the default, which is what the loader validates against (see Persistence ). Default : the literal in _default_settings . Range / Values : what the save slot enforces (clamp or whitelist), and in parentheses the range the UI control offers when it is narrower. A dash means the slot stores whatever it is given. UI : the page under frontend/settings/ (Display, Media, OBD, Accessories, Device, About) or the other QML file that writes the key. See Settings UI . Pinnable settings 25 of these keys are also registered in SETTINGS_REGISTRY and can be pinned to the Quick Panel. They are marked pinnable in the description; the registry itself is documented on the Settings Manager page. Device and Window Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values UI Description deviceName deviceName str \"Default Device\" \u2013 Device page (via Main.updateDeviceName() , which rejects blank names) Display name for this OCTAVE installation fontSetting fontSetting str \"GoogleSans Regular\" \u2013 Display page UI font family; Style.setFont() is called on change. The default is the display name Main.qml derives from the bundled GoogleSans-Regular.ttf ; a stored legacy \"Google Sans\" (which Style.setFont() rejected, leaving the system font) is migrated to it at load and written back windowState windowState str \"windowed\" \"windowed\", \"fullscreen\", \"maximized\" (others ignored) Display page (Window) Window mode applied at startup screenWidth screenWidth int 1280 \u2013 Display page; Main.qml also saves the size on window resize Window width in pixels when windowed screenHeight screenHeight int 720 \u2013 Display page; Main.qml on resize Window height in pixels when windowed Display Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values UI Description uiScale uiScale float 1.0 \u2013 (UI 0.5 \u2013 2.0, step 0.05) Display page Global UI scale factor ( Spacing.globalScale ). A stored 0.6 (an old default) is migrated to 1.0 at load. Pinnable themeSetting themeSetting str \"CosmicVoyager\" Any built-in or custom theme name; \"Album Art Capture\" enables palette extraction Display page; Main.updateTheme() Active colour theme ( Theme System ). Pinnable (chips from themeList ) customThemes customThemes dict (property is a list of names) {} name \u2192 theme object with colour keys and optional albumArt file URL Saved from MediaRoom ( save_custom_theme ), deleted from the Display page User-created themes captured from album art; art files live in theme_art/ colorTransitionMs colorTransitionMs int 1000 \u2013 (UI and registry 0 \u2013 2000, step 50) Display page Duration of theme colour transitions in ms. Pinnable songLengthTransition songLengthTransition bool false true/false Display page Animate the theme colour transition over the whole song length instead of colorTransitionMs showClock showClock bool true true/false Display page (Clock); Display widget Show the clock in the bottom bar. Pinnable clockFormat24Hour clockFormat24Hour bool true true/false Display page (Clock) 24-hour clock (false = 12-hour). Signal is clockFormatChanged clockShowSeconds clockShowSeconds bool false true/false Display page (Clock) Show seconds in the bottom-bar clock clockSize clockSize int 18 \u2013 (UI 10 \u2013 85, step 1) Display page (Clock) Manual clock font size in pixels clockSizeAuto clockSizeAuto bool true \u2013 Display page (Clock) Fit the clock to its slot in the nav bar (either orientation); when on, clockSize is ignored bottomBarOrientation bottomBarOrientation str \"bottom\" \"bottom\", \"side\" (UI chips; slot does not validate) Display page (Layout) Navigation bar along the bottom edge or the side showBottomBarMediaControls showBottomBarMediaControls bool true true/false Display page (Layout) Play/skip controls inside the navigation bar. Pinnable settingsLayoutStyle settingsLayoutStyle str \"Sidebar\" \"Carousel\", \"Sidebar\", \"Hub\", \"Dashboar"}, {"url": "settings-ui.html", "title": "Settings UI", "subtitle": "The SettingsMenu orchestrator, the six settings pages and their tile grids, the four layout engines (Grid default), the Now Playing studio, the reusable component library, widgets, persistence helpers, and how to add a page", "headings": [{"id": "orchestrator", "text": "SettingsMenu Orchestrator", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-model", "text": "Page Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "pages", "text": "The Settings Pages", "level": 2}, {"id": "tile-system", "text": "Tile Grids and the Card Popup", "level": 2}, {"id": "now-playing-studio", "text": "Now Playing Studio", "level": 2}, {"id": "layouts", "text": "Layout Engines", "level": 2}, {"id": "sidebar", "text": "Grid Layout (default)", "level": 2}, {"id": "carousel", "text": "Carousel Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "hub", "text": "Hub Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboard", "text": "Dashboard Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "components", "text": "Reusable Components", "level": 2}, {"id": "widgets", "text": "Widget System", "level": 2}, {"id": "visibility", "text": "Section Visibility", "level": 2}, {"id": "persistence", "text": "Card State and Scroll Memory", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-page", "text": "Adding a New Settings Page", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings UI - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings UI Settings UI The SettingsMenu orchestrator, the six settings pages and their tile grids, the four layout engines (Grid default), the Now Playing studio, the reusable component library, widgets, persistence helpers, and how to add a page SettingsMenu Orchestrator frontend/SettingsMenu.qml is pushed onto the StackView by the bottom bar's Settings button with initialSection set to the last visited section. It renders no settings itself; it owns the page model, the visible-page model, the navigation API, and a Loader that instantiates one of four layout engines: Item { id : settingsMenu objectName : \"settingsMenu\" required property var stackView required property var mainWindow required property string initialSection property string currentSection: \"\" property bool updateAvailable: networkManager && networkManager.updateStatus === \"update-available\" property string layoutStyle: settingsManager ? settingsManager.settingsLayoutStyle : \"Sidebar\" property bool isInitialLoad: true } function layoutSourceForStyle (style) { switch (style) { case \"Carousel\" : return \"SettingsCarouselLayout.qml\" case \"Sidebar\" : return \"SettingsSidebarLayout.qml\" case \"Hub\" : return \"SettingsHubLayout.qml\" case \"Dashboard\" : return \"SettingsDashboardLayout.qml\" default : return \"SettingsSidebarLayout.qml\" } } In Component.onCompleted it pre-loads the card-state cache ( ExpandedCards.load(settingsManager) ), builds hubModel , and adopts initialSection if it is a visible section. The layout Loader 's onLoaded hands the layout a reference to the orchestrator and a live binding to hubModel ; for the Carousel it also sets the initial currentIndex . Navigation API navigateToCategory(section) \u2014 sets currentSection , persists it with settingsManager.set_last_settings_section() , then either sets the Carousel's currentIndex or calls the layout's own navigateToCategory() . navigateToSubSection(section, sub) \u2014 delegates to the layout if it implements it (Dashboard does), otherwise falls back to the category. navigateToHub() \u2014 called by the bottom bar when Settings is tapped while already open; forwards to the layout (Hub and Dashboard return to their card grid; the default grid layout closes its open card, see Grid Layout ). A Connections on settingsLayoutStyleChanged sets isInitialLoad = false and swaps the layout; the new engine then keeps currentSection instead of restoring the saved one. A 16 ms polling Timer ( scrollToSectionTimer ) scrolls the Carousel's current page to a card found by findChildByObjectName() when pendingScrollTarget is set. Page Model pageModel is the single source of truth for the six pages: readonly property var pageModel: [ { name : \"Display\" , section : \"displaySettings\" , source : \"settings/DisplaySettingsPage.qml\" , widget : \"widgets/DisplayWidget.qml\" , icon : \"\u263c\" , iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_display.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Layout\" , \"Window\" , \"Appearance\" , \"Clock\" ] }, { name : \"Media\" , section : \"mediaSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_media.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Library\" , \"Playback\" , \"Now Playing\" ] }, { name : \"OBD\" , section : \"obdSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_obd.svg\" , group : \"Connectivity\" , subSections : [ \"Connection\" , \"Adapters\" , \"Tuning\" , \"Parameters\" ] }, { name : \"Accessories\" , section : \"accessoriesSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_accessories.svg\" , group : \"Connectivity\" , desktopOnly : true , subSections : [ \"Volume Knob\" , \"IMU Sensor\" , \"Gesture Sensor\" , \"Phone Dock\" ] }, { name : \"Device\" , section : \"deviceSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_device.svg\" , group : \"System\" , subSections : [ \"Device Name\" , \"Network\" , \"Power\" , \"Diagnostics\" ] }, { name : \"About\" , section : \"about\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_about.svg\" , group : \"System\" , subSections : [] } ] Key Purpose name Label in tabs, sidebar rows and cards section Identifier used for navigation, persistence ( lastSettingsSection ) and the visibility map source Page QML, relative to frontend/ (loaded by layouts in that directory; SettingsHubCard prepends \"../\" ) widget Dashboard widget QML, relative to frontend/settings/ icon Legacy Unicode glyph, kept only as a fallback iconSource Theme-tinted SVG built from Style.assetBase ; this is what renders group Appearance / Connectivity / System (carried in hubModel , currently informational) desktopOnly buildHubModel() would drop the page when the isAndroid context property is true (Accessories); main.py sets it to False , so the page always appears subSections Anchor names for sub-navigation. Must equal the page's tileModel titles : lookup is by objectName , which pages set from modelData.title . The Settings Pages Five of the six pages are Flickable s that declare a tileModel \u2014 an array of { cardId, title, summary, component } \u2014 and put each card's body in its own Component . The same compo"}, {"url": "signals-slots-reference.html", "title": "Signals &amp; Slots Reference", "subtitle": "Public API of every Python backend object registered as a QML context property, taken from the Property, @Slot and Signal declarations in backend/.", "headings": [{"id": "registry", "text": "Context property registry", "level": 2}, {"id": "conventions", "text": "Reading this page", "level": 2}, {"id": "settingsManager", "text": "SettingsManager &mdash; settingsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "assetStore", "text": "AssetStore &mdash; assetStore", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock &mdash; clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnosticsManager", "text": "DiagnosticsManager &mdash; diagnosticsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "networkManager", "text": "NetworkManager &mdash; networkManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "volumeController", "text": "VolumeController &mdash; volumeController", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaManager", "text": "MediaManager &mdash; mediaManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "audioAnalyzer", "text": "AudioAnalyzer &mdash; audioAnalyzer", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdManager", "text": "OBDManager &mdash; obdManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdAdapterFinder", "text": "OBDAdapterFinder &mdash; obdAdapterFinder", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32VolumeManager", "text": "ESP32VolumeManager &mdash; esp32VolumeManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryIMU", "text": "BerryIMUManager &mdash; berryIMU", "level": 2}, {"id": "gestureSensor", "text": "GestureManager &mdash; gestureSensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "androidAutoManager", "text": "AndroidAutoManager &mdash; androidAutoManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dhuCapture", "text": "DhuCapture &mdash; androidAutoManager.dhuCapture", "level": 2}, {"id": "phoneMirrorManager", "text": "PhoneMirrorManager &mdash; phoneMirrorManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloadManager", "text": "DownloadManager &mdash; downloadManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboardManager", "text": "DashboardManager &mdash; dashboardManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "perfMonitor", "text": "PerfMonitor &mdash; perfMonitor", "level": 2}], "body": "Signals & Slots Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Signals & Slots Reference Signals & Slots Reference Public API of every Python backend object registered as a QML context property, taken from the Property , @Slot and Signal declarations in backend/ . How managers reach QML Every manager is a QObject subclass instantiated in main.py and registered with engine.rootContext().setContextProperty(...) before frontend/Main.qml loads, so QML reaches it by name: mediaManager.next_track() , settingsManager.themeSetting , obdManager.onRpmChanged . Other context values are screenAutoScale (float: short screen edge / 720, clamped to 0.4\u20133.0), isAndroid (bool, always False ), wikiViewerAvailable (bool: PySide6.QtWebEngineQuick imported) and wikiHomeUrl ( QUrl of wiki/index.html ). The qmlRegisterType \u2019d OCTAVE.AndroidAuto.EmbeddedDhuItem , the dhuframe image provider and the QML singletons ( OBDParameterModel , WidgetCatalog , \u2026) are documented in Components , not here. Context property registry 17 QObject context properties, plus the dhuCapture sub-object and the optional developer profiler. The scalar context values are described above. QML name Python class Module Notes settingsManager SettingsManager backend/settings_manager.py assetStore AssetStore backend/asset_store.py clock Clock backend/clock.py diagnosticsManager DiagnosticsManager backend/diagnostics_manager.py networkManager NetworkManager backend/network_manager.py volumeController VolumeController backend/volume_utils.py mediaManager MediaManager backend/media_manager.py audioAnalyzer AudioAnalyzer backend/audio_analyzer.py obdManager OBDManager backend/obd_manager.py Registered through install_obd_manager() so dev/ can swap in a MockOBDManager obdAdapterFinder OBDAdapterFinder backend/obd_adapter_finder.py supported is true on Linux only esp32VolumeManager ESP32VolumeManager backend/esp32_volume_manager.py berryIMU BerryIMUManager backend/berryimu_manager.py gestureSensor GestureManager backend/gesture_manager.py androidAutoManager AndroidAutoManager backend/android_auto/manager.py Embedded display works on Windows only dhuCapture DhuCapture backend/android_auto/dhu_capture.py Not registered directly; reached via androidAutoManager.dhuCapture phoneMirrorManager PhoneMirrorManager backend/phone_mirror/manager.py downloadManager DownloadManager backend/download_manager.py dashboardManager DashboardManager backend/dashboard_manager.py perfMonitor PerfMonitor backend/perf_monitor.py Only with --profile ; module is gitignored developer tooling Reading this page Properties are PySide6 Property objects (mostly the @Property(type, notify=...) decorator form). Almost all are read-only from QML; call the matching save_* / set* slot to change them. Notify signal is what a QML binding or on<Name>Changed handler listens to; constant properties have none. Slots are methods decorated with @Slot(...) , the only Python methods QML can call. The signature shows the Python parameter names with the types passed to @Slot ; Returns is the result= type ( None when there is none). A plain Python method without @Slot is not callable from QML, even when main.py calls it. Signals are class attributes name = Signal(types...) ; the column lists the payload types the QML handler receives. Types are written as in the source: Python types ( str , int , float , bool , list , dict , bytes , object ) or PySide/Qt classes ( QObject , QUrl ). A quoted type such as \"QVariantList\" , \"QVariantMap\" , \"QVariant\" or \"qint64\" is a Qt meta-type name the source passes to PySide as a string; lists and maps arrive in QML as JS arrays and objects. Rows are in source order. Several stacked @Slot decorators on one method are shown as one row with each signature. SettingsManager \u2014 settingsManager backend/settings_manager.py \u2014 QML context property settingsManager Central settings store. Persists settingsConfigure.json in the app data directory (writes are debounced 500 ms and atomic; flushPendingSave() forces them). Settings with a dedicated property follow a triple: a read-only Property , a <name>Changed signal and a save_<snake_name>() slot. Types, defaults and validation for every key are in the Settings Reference ; persistence is described in Settings Manager . Properties Property Type Access Notify signal Description uiScale float read-only uiScaleChanged Global UI scale factor multiplied with screenAutoScale \u2014 Settings Reference colorTransitionMs int read-only colorTransitionMsChanged Duration of theme colour transitions in ms (registry slider 0\u20132000, step 50) \u2014 Settings Reference songLengthTransition bool read-only songLengthTransitionChanged Spread the album-art colour transition over the length of the song \u2014 Settings Reference bottomBarOrientation str read-only bottomBarOrientationChanged Navigation bar placement: \"bottom\" / \"side\" \u2014 Settings Reference showBottomBarMediaControls bool read-only showBottomBarMediaControlsChanged Show play/skip controls in the bottom bar \u2014 Settings Reference backg"}, {"url": "theme-system.html", "title": "Theme System", "subtitle": "Style.qml colour themes and fonts, Album Art Capture (k-means palettes from cover art in backend/media_manager.py), colour transitions, the single-preset EnvironmentTheme, Spacing.qml layout scaling and the GUI scale slider, and the ThemedIcon SVG tinting pipeline", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "style-qml", "text": "Style.qml &mdash; Theme Resolution", "level": 2}, {"id": "builtin-themes", "text": "Built-in Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "theme-object", "text": "Theme Object Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "theme-picker", "text": "Theme Picker and Swatch Chips", "level": 2}, {"id": "custom-themes", "text": "Custom (Saved Album Art) Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-tokens", "text": "Colour Token Reference", "level": 2}, {"id": "transitions", "text": "Colour Transitions", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-system", "text": "Font System", "level": 2}, {"id": "environment-theme", "text": "EnvironmentTheme.qml &mdash; Structural Tokens", "level": 2}, {"id": "spacing-qml", "text": "Spacing.qml &mdash; Layout Scaling", "level": 2}, {"id": "scaling-functions", "text": "Scaling Functions", "level": 2}, {"id": "gui-scale", "text": "GUI Scale Slider and Auto-Scale", "level": 2}, {"id": "themed-icon", "text": "ThemedIcon and the SVG Assets", "level": 2}, {"id": "consuming-themes", "text": "How Components Consume the Theme", "level": 2}, {"id": "customization", "text": "Customization Guide", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Theme System - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Theme System Theme System Style.qml colour themes and fonts, Album Art Capture (k-means palettes from cover art in backend/media_manager.py ), colour transitions, the single-preset EnvironmentTheme, Spacing.qml layout scaling and the GUI scale slider, and the ThemedIcon SVG tinting pipeline Overview OCTAVE's look is driven by three pragma Singleton QML objects declared in frontend/qmldir and imported everywhere as App.* ( import \".\" as App from frontend/ , import \"..\" as App from frontend/settings/ ). Files that live in frontend/ itself can also reference them unqualified ( SettingsMenu.qml writes Style.assetBase ). +----------------------+ +------------------------+ +----------------------+ | Style.qml | | EnvironmentTheme.qml | | Spacing.qml | | | | | | | | COLOURS + FONTS | | STRUCTURE (one preset) | | SIZES | | - 17 built-in themes | | - cardRadius, chip / | | - dp(), dpMin() | | - custom themes | | button / switch radii| | - scaledSize() | | - Album Art Capture | | - feature flags, all | | - scalePx() | | - ~60 colour tokens, | | false in \"Standard\" | | - percentage tokens | | each with Behavior | | | | for every screen | | - fontFamily, fonts | | | | - globalScale / auto | | - assetBase URL | | | | scale factors | +----------------------+ +------------------------+ +----------------------+ | | | +--------------------------+----------------------------+ | Wired at startup by frontend/Main.qml from settingsManager (themeSetting, fontSetting, uiScale, colorTransitionMs, ...) The Python backend owns persistence only. SettingsManager ( backend/settings_manager.py ) stores themeSetting , fontSetting , uiScale , colorTransitionMs , songLengthTransition , customThemes and albumArtColors ; MediaManager ( backend/media_manager.py ) produces Album Art Capture palettes. Everything else is QML. Style.qml \u2014 Theme Resolution frontend/Style.qml holds the built-in palettes, the custom-theme map, the live album-art palette, the font registry, and one reactive colour property per UI token. The activeTheme object is what every token binds to, resolved in priority order: property var activeTheme: { // 1. Album Art Capture with a live palette if (currentTheme === \"Album Art Capture\" && albumArtTheme) return albumArtTheme // 2. Custom themes saved by the user if (customThemes[currentTheme]) return customThemes[currentTheme] // 3. Built-in themes, with a hard fallback return themes[currentTheme] || themes[ \"SolarizedLight\" ] } Two setters exist. setTheme(name) validates the name against themes and customThemes , records lastStaticTheme (unless the name is Album Art Capture, in which case it arms _firstAlbumArtUpdate ) and assigns currentTheme , letting the Behavior s animate. setThemeInstant(name) does the same but zeroes colorTransitionMs for the duration of the switch and restores it with Qt.callLater . Main.qml uses setThemeInstant for the startup load and for every manual theme change; the Display page's chips call setTheme after persisting through settingsManager.save_theme_setting() . Other public members: getAllThemeNames() (built-ins minus \"Album Art Capture\", plus custom names), getLightThemeNames() / getDarkThemeNames() , addCustomTheme(name, obj) (emits customThemesUpdated ), updateAlbumArtTheme(json) , isAlbumArtCaptureActive , lastStaticTheme , and the read-only URL assetBase ( Qt.resolvedUrl(\"assets/\") ). Built-in Themes Seventeen palettes are defined in the themes map: eight light, eight dark, plus the \"Album Art Capture\" placeholder. The picker order below is hand-tuned in lightThemeNames / darkThemeNames (\"so the swatch palette flows nicely\"). The default on a fresh install is CosmicVoyager (the themeSetting default in backend/settings_manager.py ). Light Theme base baseAlt accent text.primary SolarizedLight #FDF6E3 #EEE8D5 #268BD2 #073642 ArcticChill #F0F4F8 #DCE5EE #0EA5E9 #0F2942 Parchment #F5EBD8 #E8DBC1 #8B4513 #2A1A0A Sunshine #FFFCF2 #FFF6D8 #F97316 #3A1E03 MintGarden #F0F9F4 #DDF0E2 #16A34A #0F2A1A RoseQuartz #FBF2F4 #F5E0E5 #BE185D #3A0E1F LavenderMist #F5F3FA #E9E4F2 #6D28D9 #1F0E3D SlateCloud #F1F5F9 #E2E8F0 #475569 #0F172A Dark Theme base baseAlt accent text.primary AmberConsole #000000 #0A0A0A #FFB000 #FFE89C NeonMatrix #0C0C0C #161616 #00FF41 #CCFFCC ForestEmerald #0A1A14 #122620 #10B981 #D1FAE5 CosmicVoyager (default) #0A0E17 #151C29 #00BFFF #E0F2FF TechnoRetro #1E1014 #2A1B22 #FF00AA #F5E0FF QuantumNebula #0F0F1A #17172A #9C27B0 #F0E6FF CrimsonEmber #1A0808 #241010 #DC2626 #FFE4E4 AutumnCascade #2D1E12 #3E2918 #E67E22 #FDF2E9 Placeholder Theme base baseAlt accent Note Album Art Capture #1a1a2e #16213e #e94560 Only used when no palette has been extracted yet; excluded from getAllThemeNames() and surfaced as a toggle, not a chip. Themes differ in how much per-button variety they carry. The themes added with the swatch redesign (commit 7ac3c31 : ArcticChill, Parchment, Sunshine, MintGarden, RoseQuartz, LavenderMist, SlateCloud, AmberConsole, plus CrimsonE"}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "subtitle": "Symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix for the Python backend, where the logs are and what is in them, and the observability built into the app for diagnosing problems after the fact.", "headings": [{"id": "first-look", "text": "Where to Look First", "level": 2}, {"id": "logs", "text": "Log Files, Crash Traces &amp; Debug Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "installation", "text": "Installation Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display &amp; Rendering Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "audio", "text": "Audio Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD Connection Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloads", "text": "Music Download (yt-dlp) Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-mirror", "text": "Phone Mirror Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "hardware-sensors", "text": "Hardware Sensor Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tooling", "text": "Dev Tooling Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "wsl", "text": "WSL Display Issues", "level": 2}], "body": "Troubleshooting - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Troubleshooting Troubleshooting Symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix for the Python backend, where the logs are and what is in them, and the observability built into the app for diagnosing problems after the fact. Where to Look First Start with the status text on the affected page and the logs. OCTAVE has no global error-notification layer, but individual views show details (for example, tap a failed download to open its error popup). These are the main places to look: The Connection Log / status text on the relevant page \u2014 the Connection Log on Settings \u2192 OBD shows each connection step and rfcomm / permission errors; the Download page shows the error for a failed item and marks its queue row failed; the phone-mirror view shows the adb / authorization message. Settings \u2192 Device \u2192 Diagnostics card ( diagnosticsManager ) \u2014 a 2\u00d72 grid: Refresh (last 200 log lines), Copy (last 400 lines to the clipboard), Export logs (copies the log files to the Downloads folder \u2014 the way to get logs off a head unit without a shell) and Open folder . The log directory path is printed on the card. The rotating log files described next. A hung UI, a crash and a dropped adapter all leave a trace there even when the screen shows nothing. The terminal \u2014 the console handler prints the same INFO+ lines (DEBUG+ with --debug ) to stdout. Log Files, Crash Traces & Debug Mode Log directory The backend writes to a logs/ subdirectory of the app data directory ( get_app_data_dir() ): Platform Path Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE\\logs\\ macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/logs/ Linux / Raspberry Pi $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE/logs/ , by default ~/.config/OCTAVE/logs/ Log files File Level Max size Backups Notes octave.log INFO+ 5 MB 3 backend/logging_config.py , RotatingFileHandler octave-error.log ERROR+ 2 MB 5 also receives unhandled-exception tracebacks and faulthandler dumps octave-debug.log DEBUG+ 10 MB 2 only with --debug Total disk is bounded at roughly 32 MB without debug, ~62 MB with it. WARNINGs (for example the UI watchdog's) are in octave.log , not in octave-error.log . Line format # Console (HH:MM:SS): HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | octave.module | message # File: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | octave.module | file.py:line | function() | message Logger names come from get_logger(__name__) : octave.obd , octave.media , octave.settings , octave.android_auto[.capture|.embedded] , octave.phone_mirror , octave.app for main.py , and octave.<module> for everything else (e.g. octave.ui_watchdog , octave.elm327_protocol , octave.phone_mirror.scrcpy_client ). python-obd's own obd logger is attached to the same handlers, so an ELM327 session leaves its write: / read: transcript in octave-debug.log (with --debug ). Many OBD messages also carry an [OBD] prefix. Crash traces sys.excepthook and threading.excepthook log Unhandled exception on the main thread / Unhandled exception in thread '<name>' with the traceback at CRITICAL. faulthandler is enabled on a descriptor of octave-error.log , so a native fault (SIGSEGV, SIGABRT, SIGFPE, SIGBUS, SIGILL \u2014 e.g. inside PySide6 or PyAV) dumps every Python thread's stack there. SIGTERM / SIGINT (launcher scripts, systemd, Ctrl-C) are turned into app.quit() , with a 500 ms no-op timer so Python gets to run the handler inside app.exec() ; the aboutToQuit cleanup then runs and child processes / adb forwards are not orphaned. UI watchdog: \"UI thread blocked for N s and counting\" A watchdog thread ( backend/ui_watchdog.py ) posts a ping to the GUI thread every 2 s; if the GUI thread has not answered within 5 s a WARNING is logged under octave.ui_watchdog , once per stall, followed by UI thread responsive again after N s when it recovers. A frozen head unit therefore leaves a timestamped trace even though nothing was drawn. Look at the lines immediately before the warning to see which manager was busy; the watchdog is stopped first in the quit cleanup so shutdown does not show as a stall. Enabling debug mode python main.py --debug Creates octave-debug.log with DEBUG-level output. Useful for: the python-obd TX/RX transcript, serial lines from the ESP32 knob, settings load/save, media library scanning and tag extraction, and the phone-mirror adb / server launch sequence. Installation Issues See Running from Source for setup.py -specific problems. All platforms Problem Solution PySide6 fails to install Use a 64-bit Python 3.10 or newer ( pyproject.toml declares >=3.10 with no upper cap and requirements.txt asks for PySide6>=6.8.0 ; CI runs Python 3.11; setup.py refuses anything below 3.10). Run pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel in the venv, then retry. setup.py recreates the venv every run It recreates a venv whose pyvenv.cfg was written on the other OS family (it checks for a C:\\ path) or whose pip --version fails \u2014 typical of a checkout synced between Linux and Windows. Keep one venv per machine, or run from a non-synced clone. venv_old_<timestamp> folders appear setup.py renames a venv it c"}, {"url": "utilities.html", "title": "Utility Modules", "subtitle": "Every supporting module of the Python backend that is not a feature manager: Clock, logging and crash handlers, the UI watchdog, DiagnosticsManager, VolumeController, version, lazy imports, main-thread dispatch, atomic file writes, AssetStore, the developer-local performance monitor and command server, and the backend/media / backend/temp directories.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview &amp; file map", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging", "level": 2}, {"id": "crash-handlers", "text": "Crash handlers &amp; signal shutdown", "level": 2}, {"id": "ui-watchdog", "text": "UI watchdog", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnostics-manager", "text": "DiagnosticsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume-controller", "text": "VolumeController", "level": 2}, {"id": "version", "text": "Version &amp; update check", "level": 2}, {"id": "lazy-import", "text": "Lazy imports", "level": 2}, {"id": "qt-threading", "text": "Main-thread dispatch (qt_threading)", "level": 2}, {"id": "atomic-io", "text": "Atomic file writes (atomic_io)", "level": 2}, {"id": "asset-store", "text": "AssetStore", "level": 2}, {"id": "perf-monitor", "text": "Performance monitor &amp; perf tooling", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command server", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-dirs", "text": "backend/media and backend/temp", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Utility Modules - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Utility Modules Utility Modules Every supporting module of the Python backend that is not a feature manager: Clock, logging and crash handlers, the UI watchdog, DiagnosticsManager, VolumeController, version, lazy imports, main-thread dispatch, atomic file writes, AssetStore, the developer-local performance monitor and command server, and the backend/media / backend/temp directories. Overview & file map The feature managers (media, OBD, sensors, phone mirror, downloads, dashboards) each have their own wiki page. This page covers everything else that supports them. The table shows where each piece lives, whether QML can reach it, and the context-property name registered in main.py . Module File QML context property Notes Clock backend/clock.py clock QObject Logging backend/logging_config.py \u2013 Not a QObject; set up first thing in main.py Crash handlers logging_config._install_crash_handlers() \u2013 Write to octave-error.log UI watchdog backend/ui_watchdog.py \u2013 (not registered) QObject, internal only DiagnosticsManager backend/diagnostics_manager.py diagnosticsManager Settings \u2192 Device \u2192 Diagnostics card VolumeController backend/volume_utils.py volumeController Quadratic curve + dispatch Version backend/version.py \u2013 (surfaced as diagnosticsManager.appVersion ) __version__ = \"0.9.5\" Lazy imports backend/lazy_import.py \u2013 Deferred imports of heavy optional packages Main-thread dispatch backend/qt_threading.py \u2013 run_on_main(fn, delay_ms=0) Atomic writes backend/atomic_io.py \u2013 atomic_write_bytes / atomic_write_text AssetStore backend/asset_store.py assetStore localUrl(relativePath) Performance monitor backend/perf_monitor.py , backend/perf_patches.py perfMonitor (only with --profile ) Developer-local (gitignored) Command server backend/command_server.py \u2013 Developer-local (gitignored), only with --profile Developer-local files that are not in a fresh clone .gitignore lists backend/perf_monitor.py , backend/perf_patches.py and backend/command_server.py under \u201cPerformance profiling (lives in dev/perf/)\u201d, together with the whole top-level dev/ tree, backend/temp/ and .mcp.json . They exist on the developer's machines but are not versioned. main.py only imports them inside setup_perf_profiling() , which runs when --profile is passed, and every one of those imports is guarded: on a checkout without them --profile logs --profile: backend/perf_monitor.py not present (gitignored dev tooling), skipping performance monitor and command server (or the command_server.py / perf_patches.py equivalent) and carries on. A normal python main.py never touches them. The descriptions of these modules below were checked against the developer's copies. Clock backend/clock.py \u2014 QML name: clock A QTimer -based clock that formats the current time once a second and emits it as a string. It reads three settings from SettingsManager on every tick \u2014 showClock , clockFormat24Hour and clockShowSeconds \u2014 so a settings change takes effect within a second without any signal wiring. It emits timeChanged only when the text actually changed : without seconds the string changes once a minute, so the QML bindings that show the clock re-evaluate 60\u00d7 less often. Hiding the clock emits the empty string once; because the last-emitted text starts as None , a clock that starts hidden also emits \"\" on its first tick. Public API Member Kind Description timeChanged(str) Signal(str) Formatted time, or \"\" when showClock is off update_time() @Slot() Timer callback (1000 ms); computes the text and emits on change Clock(settings_manager) constructor Starts the timer immediately Format Setting Without seconds With seconds clockFormat24Hour = true 14:30 ( %H:%M ) 14:30:05 clockFormat24Hour = false 02:30 PM ( %I:%M + upper-cased %p ) 02:30:05 PM showClock = false \"\" (emitted once) %p follows the process locale, so the AM/PM text is locale-dependent. def _emit_if_changed (self, text): # Without seconds the text changes once a minute; re-evaluating every # binding that shows the clock 60 times for the same string is waste. if text == self._last_text: return self._last_text = text self.timeChanged.emit(text) QML consumers frontend/BottomBar.qml \u2014 one Connections { target: clock } at bar level writes a shared clockTimeText property that both the horizontal and the vertical layout bind to, so the layout loaded on an orientation switch shows the current time at once instead of waiting for the next emit frontend/ClockMenu.qml \u2014 same signal drives the digital readout, the date line and the analog hands ( time.split(\":\") for hours / minutes / seconds; a missing seconds field, i.e. clockShowSeconds off, counts as 0) The Clock never logs (the octave.clock entry in the logger name map is unused). Logging backend/logging_config.py Rotating log files go to <app data dir>/logs/ , where the app data dir is the one that holds settingsConfigure.json (Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE , macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE , Linux $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE o"}];
